#!/usr/bin/env bash
# orphan-ok: on-demand ORGANIZATIONAL-CLOSURE poll — a diagnostic READ over the genome's own
#   dependency structure, invoked manually or by a dash refresh (like mesh-forage / mesh-entropy).
#   No cron: it measures a STATIC graph (the wired reflex set + who-invokes-whom in scripts/), it
#   does not act. It is ADVISORY — every PERIPHERAL flag is a candidate for a human's eyes, never
#   an auto-action (matching mesh-doctor's WARN discipline). An unseen reflex is absent; wiring it
#   into `mesh-dash minds` is the natural next step, left unwired on purpose.
#
# mesh-closure — does the mesh's wired-reflex set form a self-maintaining CLOSED ORGANIZATION, or a
# feed-forward pipeline with inert appendages? Reports the interior CORE (reflexes that both consume
# and are consumed), the boundary SOURCEs (pure producers), the mutual LOOPS (literal closed
# constraint pairs a<->b), and — the load-bearing output — the PERIPHERAL reflexes: wired, firing,
# maintained by their cron slot, yet whose product NO other genome tool consumes. Enabled, enabling
# nothing: outside the organization's closure.
#
# LITERATURE (live review, genome, 2026-07-28 — autopoiesis & the biology of cognition, angle = an
# OPERATIONAL mechanism, not the philosophy). Maturana & Varela's autopoiesis says a living system
# is a network of processes that produce the very components that produce the network — operational
# CLOSURE. The mechanism was made COMPUTABLE by Montevil & Mossio, "Biological organisation as
# closure of constraints" (J. Theor. Biol. 372:179-191, 2015; https://hal.science/hal-01192916v1):
# a biological organization is a set of CONSTRAINTS each of which is (a) maintained by other
# constraints in the set and (b) contributes to maintaining them — a closed loop of mutual
# dependence, distinct from the open thermodynamic flow of PROCESSES it acts on. The freshest
# computational form is the process-enablement graph: Sousa et al., "Studying self-organisation
# across the biosphere with process-enablement graphs" (arXiv:2411.17012, 2024;
# https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17012) — nodes are processes, a directed edge A->B means "A enables B",
# and a self-maintaining organization is a subgraph closed under enablement (every member enabled
# from within). That is exactly the object this tool computes over the mesh.
#
# CROSS-DOMAIN TRANSFER: the mesh's constraints are its WIRED REFLEXES (reflexes.cron); the enabling
# relation is "B's source consumes A's product" — approximated, conservatively, by "B's source
# invokes mesh-A" (command-substitution/pipe over A's output) or reads A's state artifact. A reflex
# whose product no other constraint consumes is not part of the closure: it spends maintenance (a
# cron slot, CPU, a growing log) and enables no other constraint. This is a DIFFERENT axis from the
# two checks we already have. mesh-doctor's orphan check asks "is it WIRED?" (source+crontab) — a
# PERIPHERAL reflex passes it green, it IS wired. mesh-reflex-health asks "did it FIRE?" (mtime) — a
# PERIPHERAL reflex passes that too, it fires every N minutes. Neither ever asks "does anything
# DEPEND on its output?" — the organizational-closure question autopoiesis names. A reflex can be
# wired, firing, AND organizationally inert; only this graph sees that.
#
# HONESTY BOUNDS (conservative by construction):
#   - The enablement edge is MENTION-based (any `mesh-A` token in B's source counts B as consuming
#     A), so edges are OVER-counted -> PERIPHERAL is UNDER-flagged. The peripheral list is therefore
#     HIGH-PRECISION (a flagged reflex is genuinely un-mentioned anywhere) and a LOWER BOUND on the
#     true set. It is advisory: an ACTUATOR whose consumer is the world/operator (TG, room, speaker)
#     legitimately has no in-graph consumer, and a state-file consumed only by a dashboard may read
#     as peripheral. Judge each flag; never auto-prune.
#   - A wired reflex whose SOURCE file is absent from the scanned tree -> UNKNOWN, never PERIPHERAL
#     (absence of evidence is not enabling-nothing). Honest degradation, no faked verdict.
#
# CLASSIFICATION (FALSIFIABLE via --test):
#   CORE       consumers(a) nonempty AND invokes(a) nonempty  (interior: consumes AND is consumed)
#   SOURCE     consumers(a) nonempty AND invokes(a) empty     (boundary producer: a raw sense)
#   PERIPHERAL consumers(a) EMPTY                              (product feeds no other constraint)
#   UNKNOWN    source file not found in the scanned tree
#   LOOP a<->b a in invokes(b) AND b in invokes(a)            (a literal closed constraint pair)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --timescale — LITERATURE (live review, genome, 2026-08-04; autopoiesis & the biology of
# cognition, second landing). The graph above treats an enablement edge as a BINARY fact: A is
# mentioned by B, therefore A constrains B. Montevil & Mossio's definition never was binary — a
# CONSTRAINT is an entity that acts on a process *while being CONSERVED at that process's
# timescale*. Conservation is half the definition, and this tool dropped it. The live literature
# makes exactly that omission its subject:
#
#   Lopez-Diaz & Gershenson, "A Matter of Time: Towards a General Theory of Agency",
#   arXiv:2606.23122 (v1 2026-06-22, v2 2026-07-30)
#   https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.23122 — "the precarious physical realization of self-reference is
#   necessarily diachronic": constitutive constraints ACT, DECAY and REGENERATE on DIFFERENT
#   characteristic timescales, and a closure claim is only falsifiable once it is SCALE-EXPLICIT.
#   Same lineage the graph above already cites (it reconstructs the process-enablement graph as
#   its first operational strategy) — this is the axis it adds on top.
#
# CROSS-DOMAIN TRANSFER. Every wired reflex declares its own characteristic time: its cron
# cadence. So each edge A->B carries two of them — tau_A (how often A REGENERATES its product)
# and tau_B (how often B CONSUMES it) — and the conservation criterion is readable directly:
#
#   CONSTRAINT  tau_A >= tau_B — A's product is conserved across B's cycle. B reads a settled
#               value; A genuinely constrains B. (This is the criterion SATISFIED — a constraint
#               is supposed to be the SLOW term. Slower is not a fault, it is the definition.)
#   ALIASED     tau_A <  tau_B by >= MESH_CLOSURE_ALIAS_MIN (default 2x) — A regenerates faster
#               than B ever looks. At B's timescale A is not a constraint at all but another
#               fluctuating PROCESS, and B discards (tau_B/tau_A - 1) of every tau_B/tau_A
#               regenerations A pays a cron slot to produce. The edge is in the closure graph and
#               contributes nothing to closure at the timescale where closure is claimed.
#   MARGINAL    tau_A < tau_B but under the ratio floor — noise, reported separately, not flagged.
#   UNTIMED     a cadence could not be read for one end — honest n/a, counted as NEITHER.
#
# and the loop criterion falls straight out: a mutual pair a<->b is a closed CONSTRAINT loop only
# if BOTH legs are conserved (tau_a >= tau_b AND tau_b >= tau_a), i.e. only when tau_a == tau_b.
# MATCHED loops are timescale-valid closure; SKEWED loops are closure on paper at two different
# clocks. That is the number the flat graph could not produce.
#
# DISTINCT from what we already embody: `a-lease-must-exceed-its-producers-cadence` and the
# liveness-touch convention are per-artifact FRESHNESS rules (one consumer, one TTL, is the value
# stale?). This is an EDGE property of the closure graph and an aggregate over it — an ALIASED
# edge is never stale (the consumer always reads a fresh value); the waste is on the PRODUCER's
# side, and no freshness rule can see it. It is also the inverse of PERIPHERAL: not "nobody
# consumes you" but "your one consumer looks at 1 in N of what you make".
#
# HONESTY BOUNDS (on top of the mention-based ones above):
#   - The period is the MEAN inter-fire interval over a WEEK (10080 / fires-per-week). A bursty
#     schedule (`*/5 * * * 1` — every 5 min, Mondays only) therefore reports its weekly mean
#     (35m), not its burst rate (5m). Stated, not hidden; the fixture asserts exactly that value.
#   - The scheduled tool of a cron line is taken to be its FIRST mesh-* token. A reflex that
#     never appears as a first token has NO cadence -> its edges are UNTIMED, never guessed.
#   - dom+dow both restricted is cron's OR; approximated by the dow leg alone.
#   - Report-only. Advisory like the rest of the tool: it never prunes, never edits cron.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --symmetry — LITERATURE (live review, genome, 2026-08-04; autopoiesis & the biology of
# cognition, third landing). Both axes above are STRUCTURAL: they read the genome's *text* (who
# mentions whom) and the cron table (how often), and from those two static facts pronounce on
# closure. Neither ever looks at whether the organization and its activity actually MOVE together
# over time. That is a different claim, and the field now has an empirical test for it.
#
# Live cite: **Anthony Gosme, "Causal symmetrization as an empirical signature of operational
# autonomy in complex systems", arXiv:2512.09352** (v2, submitted 2025-12-09;
# https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.09352). The paper's move: an operationally-closed system is defined
# by RECIPROCAL constraint between form and function, so closure should leave a *directional*
# fingerprint in the joint dynamics of (structure, activity). Measured over 50 collaborative
# software ecosystems / 11,042 system-months, the structure<->activity Granger-causality coupling
# ratio shifts from **0.71 (activity-driven)** in the exploratory regime to **0.94 (bidirectional)**
# at maturity — "causal symmetrization" — alongside a 1.77-fold variance collapse. Their structure
# index is Gamma = structural survival x content survival (persistence of an edit at a horizon).
# The claim is deliberately weak-but-testable: symmetrization is a NECESSARY statistical signature
# consistent with operational closure, "not implying biological life or mechanistic closure", and
# Granger causality "establishes predictive precedence, not generative mechanism".
#
# WHY IT IS NEW HERE: everything the mesh has built on closure is an ASSERTION ABOUT STRUCTURE —
# this tool's enablement graph (does A's product reach B?), --timescale (is it conserved at B's
# clock?), mesh-vitality's allopoiesis_gap (how long does the land->deploy loop stay open?),
# heteronomy_index (who pays for cognition?). None of them asks the dynamical question: over time,
# does the constraint network PREDICT the work, or only FOLLOW it? A genome can score CORE on every
# static edge and still be purely activity-driven — tools changed only when work demands it, never
# themselves shaping what gets done next. That system has structural closure and no operational
# closure, and until now nothing here could tell the two apart.
#
# CROSS-DOMAIN TRANSFER (our two series, from the genome's own git history):
#   ACTIVITY A_t = log1p(lines changed in bin t)              — collective throughput (their "activity")
#   STRUCTURE S_t = Gamma_t, mean persistence of the edits made in that bin: an edit SURVIVES (1.0)
#     if its file is not touched again within the horizon tau, scores 0.8 if the only thing that
#     happens to it inside tau is a pure RELOCATION (rename with no churn — the paper's lambda=0.8),
#     and 0 if it is rewritten. Renames are chained (old->new) so a moved file is not a dead file.
#   Then: g(S->A) and g(A->S), the log-likelihood-ratio Granger statistics ln(RSS_restricted /
#   RSS_unrestricted) from a bivariate VAR, lag L chosen by AIC over a fixed sample, and the
#   symmetry RATIO g(S->A)/g(A->S) in the paper's orientation.
#
# THE LEAKAGE GUARD (load-bearing — without it this axis measures itself). Gamma_t is computed from
# what happens in [t, t+tau]: it is a FORWARD-looking quantity. Published at bin t it would carry
# future activity inside it, and g(S->A) would be inflated by construction — a difference whose sign
# is guaranteed. So Gamma is published at the bin where its horizon CLOSES (t + tau/bin), i.e. at
# the moment the verdict is first knowable. Measured on this genome 2026-08-04, the guard is worth
# the whole verdict: unguarded g(S->A)=0.0497 p=0.020 -> SYMMETRIZED; guarded 0.0063 p=0.675 ->
# ACTIVITY-DRIVEN. The naive form was reading its own look-ahead as structure causing work, and it
# would have reported the mesh operationally closed on the strength of it.
#
# HONESTY BOUNDS:
#   - Significance is an EXACT circular-shift permutation test on the source series (all n-1 shifts,
#     p = (1+#{g_k >= g_obs})/(1+n_shifts)) — not a parametric F-test. Both series are strongly
#     autocorrelated and a parametric p is anticonservative there; a circular shift preserves the
#     autocorrelation and destroys only the cross-alignment. Deterministic: no seed, no sampling.
#   - Diurnal common drive is removed first (per-bin-of-day mean subtracted from both series); a
#     shared day/night cycle would otherwise manufacture coupling in BOTH directions.
#   - A series with lag-1 autocorrelation >= 0.9 is first-differenced (near-unit-root heuristic, NOT
#     an ADF test — reported in the output as dS/dA so the reader knows which was transformed).
#   - The verdict is SIGNIFICANCE-based, not the paper's 0.71/0.94 cut-points: those are calibrated
#     on 50 software ecosystems binned by month, and importing a threshold across substrates is how
#     a constant outlives its reader. The ratio is REPORTED as a continuous index; the label comes
#     from which directions survive their own null.
#   - Granger = predictive precedence, not mechanism (the paper says so; so do we).
#   - The two knobs (bin width, horizon tau) move the numbers, so the verdict was swept before it
#     was believed: over bin in {4,6,8,12}h x tau in {24,48,72}h on this genome, 7/12 cells read
#     ACTIVITY-DRIVEN and 5/12 DECOUPLED — never SYMMETRIZED, never STRUCTURE-DRIVEN, and
#     g(A->S) > g(S->A) in 11 of 12. The direction of the asymmetry is the robust part; the
#     significance of the weaker leg is not. Long tau (72h) washes the axis out (fewer independent
#     survival verdicts per bin) — that is the boundary of the measurement, not a finding.
#   - Report-only, like the rest of the tool. It never prunes, never edits cron, never gates.
#
# CLASSIFICATION (FALSIFIABLE via --test against four synthetic series of KNOWN direction):
#   SYMMETRIZED     both directions beat their null — reciprocal constraint (the closure signature)
#   ACTIVITY-DRIVEN only A->S — the constraint network follows the work and does not shape it
#   STRUCTURE-DRIVEN only S->A — work follows the network, no feedback into the network
#   DECOUPLED       neither — structure and activity are two processes sharing a repo
#   UNDETERMINED    too few bins / degenerate regression (honest n/a, never a faked verdict)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --enacted — LITERATURE (live review, genome, 2026-08-15; enactivism & 4E cognition, entered from
# the angle of a foundational idea applied too loosely HERE). Every axis above defines the
# enablement edge A->B as "B's SOURCE mentions mesh-A" (see the mention-based honesty bound). The
# enactive theory of habit says that relation is not a relation at all unless it is EXERCISED:
#
#   Di Paolo, Buhrmann & Barandiaran, *Sensorimotor Life: An Enactive Proposal* (OUP 2017, p. 144)
#   define a habit as a "self-sustaining precarious sensorimotor scheme", where PRECARIOUS means
#   -- verbatim -- that "the elements that support it ... depend for their structural stability on
#   the exercise of the scheme", and SELF-SUSTAINING that "a habit 'calls' for its exercise and its
#   exercise in turn reinforces its durability" (the path worn across a lawn).
#   Unpacked, quoted and extended to longer timescales by: Susana Ramirez-Vizcaya, "Extending the
#   enactive concept of habit: from sensorimotor schemes to regional identities", Synthese 206:137
#   (2025), doi:10.1007/s11229-025-05237-7 (open access) — read 2026-08-15. Companion mechanism:
#   Ramirez-Vizcaya & Froese, Front. Psychol. 10:301 (2019), doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00301 —
#   "habits influence the viability of other habits".
#
# THE MISREAD: a `mesh-A` token sitting in a comment block, a usage line, or a --test fixture is
# structurally stable NO MATTER WHETHER THE EDGE IS EVER EXERCISED. Under the enactive criterion it
# is therefore the opposite of a habit relation — support that does not depend on exercise. This
# tool nonetheless counts it as enablement, and every closure number it has ever printed (CORE,
# LOOPS, and by subtraction PERIPHERAL) rests on that count. It is the mesh's own standing rule in
# a new place: source text is never behaviour (CLAUDE.md, the self-matching-grep lesson), and a
# name-only source gate passes on a comment.
#
# CROSS-DOMAIN TRANSFER: recompute the SAME graph three times over three mention populations and
# diff them — declared (any mention) / code (outside full-line comments) / LIVE (also outside
# test-harness function bodies). An edge present in `declared` but absent from `live` is
# UNEXERCISED: PROSE-only (the mention is documentation) or TEST-only (only the gate walks it).
# The enacted graph is a SUBGRAPH of the declared one, so classes can only weaken: a reflex that
# was CORE on prose alone falls to PERIPHERAL, and a LOOP with one unexercised leg is not a closed
# constraint pair. DEMOTED is the load-bearing list.
#
# HONESTY BOUNDS (all conservative in the SAME direction — demotions are a LOWER bound):
#   - Only a line that is ENTIRELY a comment counts as prose; a trailing-comment mention
#     (`x=1  # like mesh-foo`) is counted as live code. Live edges are never lost.
#   - A test body is recognised only when the harness is a NAMED function whose name has an exact
#     `_`-separated component test/tests/testing/smoke/selftest (the genome's do_test/_test/
#     smoke_test/run_test/selftest/ts_selftest family). A tool that inlines its gate under a
#     `--test)` case branch keeps those mentions LIVE -> TEST-only is UNDER-counted. `parse_latest`
#     contains "test" as a substring and is deliberately NOT matched.
#   - The graph reads SCRIPTS only. A tool consumed by a cron COMMAND CHAIN (`mesh-load-gate x 11
#     && mesh-y`) has a real consumer no axis of this tool scans — so its demotion is about scripts
#     alone. Those are MARKED `[cron-chained]`, never suppressed: an unmarked demotion is the strong
#     claim (live 2026-08-15: 74 of 79 are unmarked), a marked one is a caveat for the reader.
#   - A live mention is still only a mention: it proves the token appears on a line that COULD run,
#     never that it ran. This axis strictly narrows the over-count; it does not remove it. The
#     residual would need runtime tracing, which no reflex here carries.
#   - Report-only, advisory, like every other axis. It never prunes, never edits cron.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --semantic — LITERATURE (live review, genome, 2026-08-16; autopoiesis & the biology of cognition,
# entered from the angle of a concrete METRIC the field uses to measure itself).
#
#   Amahury Jafet Lopez-Diaz & Carlos Gershenson, "Closing the loop: how semantic closure enables
#   open-ended evolution?", *J. R. Soc. Interface* 22(233):20250784 (2025) — PubMed 41537873;
#   preprint arXiv:2404.04374v7 (https://arxiv.org/html/2404.04374v7), read 2026-08-16.
#   Lineage: Pattee's semantic closure -> Rosen (M,R)-systems -> Hofmeyr (F,A)-systems, given a
#   temporal parametrization here. Same author pair as arXiv:2606.23122, already cited by
#   --timescale above; this is a DIFFERENT criterion from that paper's constraint conservation.
#
# THE CRITERION. Autopoiesis asks whether the system produces its own COMPONENTS. Semantic closure
# asks a sharper question one level up: does the system produce its own INTERPRETERS — the
# constraints that map a signal to a functional consequence? The paper's ladder makes it a
# measurable, not a philosophy. Each rung is a formal-language class, and the rung is set by WHERE
# the interpreter comes from:
#
#   rung 1  finite automaton / regular          interpreter imposed by the environment (geochemistry)
#   rung 2  automaton with memory / ctx-free    loop not self-closing; interpreter still external
#   rung 3  stream X-machine / ctx-sensitive    partially intrinsic, not self-CONSTRUCTING
#   rung 4  semantically closed                 "symbols actively construct and interpret their own
#                                                functional contexts" — the system manufactures its
#                                                own measurement and control apparatus
#
#   Verbatim on the boundary: a system whose interpreters are provided from outside "remains
#   computationally simulable" and is "robust only while the environment keeps supplying" them;
#   only self-construction gives robust self-replication and open-endedness.
#
# WHERE THE MESH SITS, AND WHY THIS IS NOT ALREADY MEASURED. Every axis of closure the mesh already
# owns is about the PRODUCER of a component or a commit: allopoiesis_gap (who closes the land loop),
# heteronomy_index (who pays for the cognition), this tool's own graph/timescale/symmetry (which
# reflex enables which). NOT ONE of them asks who wrote the THRESHOLD. Yet a sense's threshold is
# exactly an interpreter in Pattee's sense: `[ "$temp" -gt 82 ]` is the constraint that turns a
# number into "hot", and 82 was chosen by an author outside the organization. Nothing regenerates
# it, nothing measures whether it still cuts where the node's own data cuts. Live failures of this
# exact shape are already on the record and were each found by hand, one at a time:
# stress-thermal-bands-calibrated-to-a-dead-regime, a-decode-table-copied-from-memory-baselines-
# away-the-alarm, a-constant-outlives-its-reader, and CLAUDE.md's own soundscape rule ("calibrate a
# derived axis against the REAL corpus, never an assumed 0..1 ... a median pinned as a constant
# ROTS"). The doctrine exists; the METRIC did not. This is it.
#
# WHAT IS COUNTED. A DECISION SITE is a numeric comparison in a tool's shell body — `-gt/-ge/-lt/-le`
# in a test, or `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` inside `(( ))`. The compared-against operand is the interpreter,
# and it is classified by PROVENANCE:
#
#   SELF        the operand traces to a runtime read of the mesh's OWN record (a command
#               substitution touching a mesh-* tool or a .mesh/ / .log / .tsv / .jsonl artifact) —
#               an interpreter the organization manufactured. The read may be ONE HOP away, inside
#               a function of the same file, and one ALIAS away (`W="$W_ABS"`); both are followed,
#               or a self-calibrated organ reports as untraceable.
#   IMPOSED     a numeric literal at the site, or a variable whose EVERY assignment is a literal, a
#               `${ENV:-<number>}` default, or a threshold-LEDGER lookup — freestanding, authored
#               outside the organization (an env override is still external instruction, and a
#               constant read out of a data row is still the constant a human wrote)
#   UNRESOLVED  the operand is a variable this scan cannot trace to either (honest n/a, never folded
#               into a class to make the number look better)
#   TRIVIAL     the cut-point is 0 or 1 — a presence/emptiness test has no calibratable degree of
#               freedom, so it is not an interpreter at all. Excluded from SCI and reported.
#
#   SCI = SELF / (SELF + IMPOSED)   over non-trivial, traceable sites.
#   rung: IMPOSED (SCI=0) · PARTIAL (0<SCI<1) · CLOSED (SCI=1) · n/a (no assessable site)
#
# HONESTY BOUNDS (the blind spot is REPORTED as a number, not left silent):
#   - The scan reads SHELL comparisons only. A tool that computes a robust baseline inside an
#     embedded awk/python body and cuts on it there (mesh-ambient-shift's median+MAD,
#     mesh-algedonic's trailing-median habituation) is genuinely PARTIALLY self-calibrated and this
#     scan cannot see it. Tools with no traceable non-trivial site but an embedded awk/python/jq
#     interpreter body are counted as OPAQUE and named; a tool that HAS sites may still hold further
#     interpreters inside such a body. So SCI is a claim about the SHELL-VISIBLE interpreters, and
#     it under-counts SELF. It is stated that way everywhere it is printed.
#   - IMPOSED is conservative by construction: a variable is IMPOSED only when EVERY assignment in
#     the file is a constant expression; any untraceable assignment demotes it to UNRESOLVED. The
#     IMPOSED count is therefore a LOWER bound and the "deepest debt" list is high-precision.
#   - Full-line comments are prose and never scanned (the same rule --enacted uses), so a threshold
#     quoted in a header block cannot inflate the count.
#   - PROVENANCE IS NOT APPROPRIATENESS. A literal can be exactly right (a protocol constant, a
#     percentage bound, 100 degrees). This axis measures WHO WROTE THE INTERPRETER, never whether
#     the value is correct. It is report-only, advisory, and judges nothing.
#
# LIVE (mesh-home, 2026-08-16, the GENOME tree scripts/, 637 tools — invoked from ~/.local/bin it
# censuses the 643 DEPLOYED ones instead and reads slightly differently; name the corpus):
# SELF=6 IMPOSED=1068 UNRESOLVED=459, TRIVIAL=888 excluded, OPAQUE=175. **SCI = 0.0056** over 1074
# assessable sites, rung PARTIAL. Re-derive, never quote: `mesh-closure --semantic`. Three of the six are gate assertions inside smoke probes (mesh-chat
# compares its chat.log line count before/after a post; mesh-dispatch compares two line numbers it
# greps out of its own source) and are not organ interpreters. The other THREE are, and they are one
# pattern repeated: mesh-node-health's PSI_STALE_S/PSIMEM_STALE_S and mesh-window-state's
# WIFI_STALE_S each read the LIVE CRONTAB and set the lease to 2x the producer's own stride. The
# mesh has exactly one working idiom of semantic closure and it is `a-lease-must-exceed-its-
# producers-cadence` — a cut-point nobody authored, derived from the organization's own schedule.
#
# The first reading of this axis said SELF=3 / SCI=0.0030 and concluded "the production reading is
# SCI = 0, rung 1, the whole sensorium". That was the SCAN's limit, not the mesh's: provenance
# stopped at the assignment RHS, and the read is normally ONE HOP away (the site says `$(band)`, the
# BODY touches the record) or one ALIAS away (`WARM="$WARM_ABS"; SRC=absolute` — a compound line the
# assignment matcher could not even parse). Both hops are traced now (fs7/fs9/fs10), which is also
# why IMPOSED ROSE: compound assignments were invisible, so the denominator was under-counted too.
# The deepest debt is mesh-dash 43, mesh-light 31, mesh-dispatch 23, mesh-socket-state 23,
# mesh-stress 21, mesh-ambient-clock 17, mesh-ideate 17, mesh-sensorium 16. It is a MEASUREMENT of a
# thing the mesh already knew case-by-case and never counted: every
# `stress-thermal-bands-calibrated-to-a-dead-regime` was found by a human noticing one constant.
#
# WHAT DID NOT MOVE, DELIBERATELY: the 20 tools that resolve a default through the uxn
# threshold-ledger (`mesh-sexpr-gate --const`) stay IMPOSED. Externalizing a magic number to a data
# row is a real engineering good — recalibration becomes a diff on the ledger, not a script edit —
# and it is NOT closure: nobody in the organization PRODUCED the 90, it moved from a script line to
# a table row. Counting the hop as SELF would have minted 30+ SELF sites in one commit and made SCI
# a measure of indirection. mesh-stress is the case that forced the call: its WARM band is genuinely
# self-calibrated (min(ledger, this node's own therm.log p50 + delta)) and it STILL reads UNRESOLVED
# here, because the p50 arm enters through `read -r CURVE_P50 … <<EOF` and this scan does not follow
# a heredoc. A named blind spot beats a flattering class.
#
# WHY THAT NUMBER IS NOT AN INDICTMENT. The paper's own claim is conditional -- an imposed
# interpreter is fine "while the environment keeps supplying it". A mesh whose thresholds are
# authored by minds and an operator is exactly that: robust as long as the authors keep re-deriving
# them. What SCI names is the DEPENDENCE, and it makes the recalibration frontier a ranked list
# instead of a memory of past incidents.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Usage:
#   mesh-closure                 classify the wired reflex set; print summary + PERIPHERAL + LOOPS
#   mesh-closure --peripheral    just the PERIPHERAL candidate list (one per line)
#   mesh-closure --timescale     constraint-CONSERVATION over the same graph (ALIASED edges, loops)
#   mesh-closure --enacted       EXERCISE test on every edge (prose-only / test-only -> demotions)
#   mesh-closure --null          degree-preserving NULL for the closure numbers (which can fail?)
#   mesh-closure --symmetry      DYNAMICAL closure: structure<->activity causal symmetrization
#   mesh-closure --symmetry-series  the raw "<bin> <gamma> <activity>" series behind it (debug)
#   mesh-closure --semantic      SEMANTIC closure: who wrote each sense's decision threshold
#   mesh-closure --semantic-sites  the raw "<tool> <class> <operand>" site stream behind it (debug)
#   mesh-closure --cadences      the parsed cron period (minutes) per wired reflex
#   mesh-closure --json          machine-readable summary
#   mesh-closure --test          smoke (synthetic fixture graph; RED-first falsifier)
#
# Env overrides (for --test and node-local tuning):
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS  genome scripts/ dir to scan   (default: repo scripts/, else ~/lte-workstation/scripts)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_CRON     wired-reflex cron file         (default: ~/.mesh/reflexes.cron)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_ALIAS_MIN  tau_B/tau_A ratio at/above which an edge is ALIASED (default 2)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_ENACTED_TOP  how many unexercised edges --enacted prints (default 15; rest announced)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_R       --null replicates (default 200; p floor is 1/(R+1))
#   MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SEED    --null RNG seed (default 20260818; runs are reproducible)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SWAPS   --null double-edge swaps per replicate, as a multiple of E (default 10)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_TIMEOUT --null python budget in seconds (default 180)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_REPO   git repo whose history feeds --symmetry (default: parent of SCRIPTS)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_BIN_H  bin width in hours   (default 6)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_TAU_H  survival horizon, hours (default 48)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_DAYS   history window, days (default 70)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_LMAX   max VAR lag for AIC selection (default 4)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_ALPHA  permutation-test alpha (default 0.05)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_MIN_N  minimum usable bins, else UNDETERMINED (default 40)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG  fixture: raw `git log --pretty='C %at' --numstat` stream (--test)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES  fixture: prebuilt "<bin> <gamma> <activity>" series (--test)
#     Both fixture vars are STRICT: set-but-unreadable is an error, never a fall-back to live git.
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TOP     how many deepest-debt tools --semantic prints (default 12; rest announced)
#   MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TRIVIAL space-separated cut-points that are NOT interpreters (default "0 1")
set -o pipefail

# --- locate the genome scripts/ tree (SOURCE, not the deployed ~/.local/bin copy) ---
_default_scripts() {
  local self d
  self="$(readlink -f "$0" 2>/dev/null || echo "$0")"
  d="$(dirname "$self")"
  [ -f "$d/mesh-closure" ] && [ -d "$d" ] && { echo "$d"; return; }   # running from a scripts/ tree
  for c in "$HOME/lte-workstation/scripts" "$HOME/lte-workstation/scripts/"; do
    [ -d "$c" ] && { echo "$c"; return; }
  done
  echo "$HOME/lte-workstation/scripts"
}
SCRIPTS="${MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS:-$(_default_scripts)}"
CRON="${MESH_CLOSURE_CRON:-$HOME/.mesh/reflexes.cron}"

# --- mention SITES: the tokens of one source file, filtered by whether the mention is EXERCISED ---
# mode=all   every `mesh-*` token anywhere in the file (the declared graph — what this tool always did)
# mode=code  tokens outside FULL-LINE comments (prose dropped)
# mode=live  tokens outside full-line comments AND outside a test/smoke FUNCTION body (the enacted graph)
# Conservative by construction in ONE direction: only a line that is ENTIRELY a comment is treated as
# prose, so a trailing-comment mention (`x=1  # see mesh-foo`) is counted as live code. A live edge can
# therefore never be miscounted as unexercised -> the DEMOTED list is a high-precision LOWER bound.
mention_tokens() {
  local f="$1" mode="${2:-all}"
  if [ "$mode" = all ]; then grep -oE 'mesh-[a-z0-9-]+' "$f" 2>/dev/null | sort -u; return 0; fi
  awk -v MODE="$mode" '
    function emit(s) { while (match(s, /mesh-[a-z0-9-]+/)) { print substr(s, RSTART, RLENGTH)
                                                             s = substr(s, RSTART + RLENGTH) } }
    # a test-harness function is recognised by an exact _-separated COMPONENT, never a substring:
    # parse_latest() contains "test" and must stay live, or real edges vanish and demotions inflate.
    function is_test_fn(nm,   p, i, k) {
      k = split(nm, p, "_")
      for (i = 1; i <= k; i++)
        if (p[i] == "test" || p[i] == "tests" || p[i] == "testing" || p[i] == "smoke" || p[i] == "selftest")
          return 1
      return (nm ~ /selftest$/)
    }
    /^[ \t]*#/ { next }                                   # a full-line comment is PROSE: never exercised
    MODE == "live" && !intest && /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*\(\)[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/ {
      nm = $0; sub(/[ \t]*\(\).*/, "", nm); gsub(/^[ \t]+/, "", nm)
      if (is_test_fn(nm)) { intest = 1; next } }
    MODE == "live" && intest { if ($0 ~ /^\}[ \t]*$/) intest = 0; next }
    { emit($0) }
  ' "$f" 2>/dev/null | sort -u
}

# --- core analysis: emit "TOOL CLASS" lines + trailing "LOOP a b" lines on stdout ---
analyze() {
  local scripts="$1" cron="$2" want_edges="${3:-0}" site_mode="${4:-all}"
  [ -d "$scripts" ] || { echo "mesh-closure: scripts dir not found: $scripts" >&2; return 3; }
  [ -f "$cron" ]    || { echo "mesh-closure: cron file not found: $cron" >&2; return 3; }

  # W = wired reflex tools (mesh-* tokens on non-comment cron lines)
  local -A wired=()
  local t
  while IFS= read -r t; do [ -n "$t" ] && wired["$t"]=1; done < <(
    grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*#' "$cron" 2>/dev/null | grep -oE 'mesh-[a-z0-9-]+' | sort -u )

  # invokes[b] = space-list of OTHER mesh-* names mentioned in b's source (edge: they enable b)
  local -A invokes=() present=()
  local f b tok seen
  for f in "$scripts"/mesh-*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    b="$(basename "$f")"
    present["$b"]=1
    seen=" "
    while IFS= read -r tok; do
      [ "$tok" = "$b" ] && continue
      case "$seen" in *" $tok "*) ;; *) seen="$seen$tok "; esac
    done < <(mention_tokens "$f" "$site_mode")
    invokes["$b"]="$seen"
  done

  # consumers[a] count = how many present tools mention a (a enables them)
  local -A ncons=()
  for b in "${!invokes[@]}"; do
    for tok in ${invokes[$b]}; do
      ncons["$tok"]=$(( ${ncons[$tok]:-0} + 1 ))
    done
  done

  # classify each wired reflex
  local a has_in has_out cls
  for a in $(printf '%s\n' "${!wired[@]}" | sort); do
    if [ -z "${present[$a]:-}" ]; then echo "$a UNKNOWN"; continue; fi
    [ "${ncons[$a]:-0}" -gt 0 ] && has_in=1 || has_in=0     # someone consumes a
    seen="${invokes[$a]}"; [ "${seen// /}" != "" ] && has_out=1 || has_out=0  # a consumes someone
    if   [ "$has_in" -eq 0 ]; then cls=PERIPHERAL
    elif [ "$has_out" -eq 1 ]; then cls=CORE
    else cls=SOURCE; fi
    echo "$a $cls"
  done

  # mutual LOOPs a<->b among wired reflexes (a in invokes[b] AND b in invokes[a]); print once (a<b)
  for a in $(printf '%s\n' "${!wired[@]}" | sort); do
    [ -n "${present[$a]:-}" ] || continue
    for b in ${invokes[$a]}; do
      [ -n "${wired[$b]:-}" ] || continue
      [ -n "${present[$b]:-}" ] || continue
      [[ "$a" < "$b" ]] || continue
      case " ${invokes[$b]} " in *" $a "*) echo "LOOP $a $b" ;; esac
    done
  done

  # EDGE lines (opt-in, --timescale only): "EDGE <producer> <consumer>" for every enablement edge
  # whose BOTH ends are wired reflexes present in the tree — the only edges that carry two cadences.
  [ "$want_edges" = 1 ] || return 0
  for b in $(printf '%s\n' "${!wired[@]}" | sort); do
    [ -n "${present[$b]:-}" ] || continue
    for tok in ${invokes[$b]}; do
      [ -n "${wired[$tok]:-}" ] || continue
      [ -n "${present[$tok]:-}" ] || continue
      echo "EDGE $tok $b"
    done
  done
}

# --- cadence: "<tool> <period-in-minutes>" for every reflex the cron actually SCHEDULES ---
# period = mean inter-fire interval over a week = 10080 / fires-per-week (see HONESTY BOUNDS).
cadences() {
  awk '
    # count the distinct values a cron field expands to within [lo,hi]
    function fcount(f, lo, hi,   n, parts, i, it, step, a, b, v, rng, c, set) {
      delete set; c = 0
      n = split(f, parts, ",")
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        it = parts[i]; step = 1
        if (match(it, /\/[0-9]+$/)) { step = substr(it, RSTART + 1) + 0; it = substr(it, 1, RSTART - 1) }
        if (step < 1) step = 1
        if (it == "*")                      { a = lo;          b = hi }
        else if (split(it, rng, "-") == 2)  { a = rng[1] + 0;  b = rng[2] + 0 }
        else                                { a = it + 0;      b = a }
        if (a < lo) a = lo
        if (b > hi) b = hi
        for (v = a; v <= b; v += step) set[v] = 1
      }
      for (v in set) c++
      return c
    }
    /^[ \t]*#/ { next }
    /^[ \t]*$/ { next }
    NF < 6     { next }
    # NB: @reboot/@daily lines are NOT special-cased. They fall out of the arithmetic below —
    # their non-numeric "fields" expand to an empty set, fires becomes 0, and the fires<=0 guard
    # drops them. A dedicated `$1 ~ /^@/ {next}` rule was removed BECAUSE the fixture proved it
    # could not fail: deleting it changed no output, so it was a gate leg asserting nothing. The
    # fires<=0 guard is the real one, and mesh-fp7 in --test is its RED-first witness.
    {
      cm = fcount($1, 0, 59); ch = fcount($2, 0, 23)
      if ($5 != "*")      dpw = fcount($5, 0, 7)          # dow wins (dom+dow is cron OR — see bounds)
      else if ($3 != "*") dpw = 7.0 * fcount($3, 1, 31) / 30.44
      else                dpw = 7
      if (dpw > 7) dpw = 7
      if ($4 != "*") dpw = dpw * fcount($4, 1, 12) / 12.0
      fires = cm * ch * dpw
      if (fires <= 0) next
      period = 10080.0 / fires
      if (!match($0, /mesh-[a-z0-9-]+/)) next             # first mesh-* token = the scheduled tool
      tool = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH)
      if (!(tool in best) || period < best[tool]) best[tool] = period
    }
    END { for (t in best) printf "%s %.4f\n", t, best[t] }
  ' "$1" | sort
}

# --- timescale render: conservation of each enablement edge, and of each mutual loop ---
render_timescale() {
  local lines; lines="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON" 1)" || return $?
  local amin="${MESH_CLOSURE_ALIAS_MIN:-2}"
  local out
  out="$( { cadences "$CRON" | sed 's/^/TAU /'; grep -E '^(EDGE|LOOP) ' <<<"$lines"; } | awk -v AMIN="$amin" '
    function m(x) { return (x == int(x) ? sprintf("%dm", x) : sprintf("%.1fm", x)) }
    $1 == "TAU"  { tau[$2] = $3 + 0; next }
    $1 == "EDGE" {
      a = $2; b = $3
      if (!(a in tau) || !(b in tau)) { untimed++; next }
      if (tau[a] >= tau[b]) { cons++; next }
      r = tau[b] / tau[a]
      if (r >= AMIN) { alias++; printf "ALIAS %.3f %s %s %s %s\n", r, a, m(tau[a]), b, m(tau[b]) }
      else marg++
      next }
    $1 == "LOOP" {
      a = $2; b = $3
      if (!(a in tau) || !(b in tau)) { luntimed++; next }
      if (tau[a] == tau[b]) matched++; else skewed++
      next }
    END { printf "SUM %d %d %d %d %d %d %d\n", cons+0, alias+0, marg+0, untimed+0, matched+0, skewed+0, luntimed+0 }
  ' )"

  local c al mg un mt sk lu
  read -r _ c al mg un mt sk lu < <(grep '^SUM ' <<<"$out")
  local assessable=$(( c + al + mg ))
  echo "mesh-closure --timescale: constraint CONSERVATION over the enablement graph"
  echo "  assessable edges=$assessable (both ends wired with a readable cadence)  UNTIMED=$un (honest n/a)"
  echo "  CONSTRAINT=$c  (tau_producer >= tau_consumer — product conserved across the consumer's cycle)"
  echo "  ALIASED=$al    (producer regenerates >= ${amin}x faster than its consumer reads — at the"
  echo "                 consumer's timescale it is a PROCESS, not a constraint; most of what it"
  echo "                 produces is never seen)"
  echo "  MARGINAL=$mg   (faster, but under the ${amin}x floor)"
  echo "  LOOPS: MATCHED=$mt (tau_a == tau_b — timescale-valid closed pair)  SKEWED=$sk (closure at two clocks)  UNTIMED=$lu"
  if [ "$al" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  worst aliasing (consumer sees ~1 of every N regenerations):"
    # NO `head` here on purpose: `set -o pipefail` + an early-exiting head SIGPIPEs `sort` and the
    # whole render would return 141 while printing a correct report. awk does the truncation and
    # ANNOUNCES the remainder — a silent top-N reads as "that's all of them" when it is not.
    grep '^ALIAS ' <<<"$out" | sort -k2,2gr \
      | awk -v N="${MESH_CLOSURE_ALIAS_TOP:-12}" '
          { n++; if (n <= N) printf "    - %s (%s) -> %s (%s)   1 in %.0f\n", $3, $4, $5, $6, $2 }
          END { if (n > N) printf "    ... and %d more aliased edge(s) (MESH_CLOSURE_ALIAS_TOP=%d)\n", n - N, N }'
  fi
}

# --- --enacted: does the enablement edge survive the EXERCISE test? ----------------------------
render_enacted() {
  local dec ena
  dec="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON" 1 all)"  || return $?
  ena="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON" 1 live)" || return $?
  local cod; cod="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON" 1 code)" || return $?

  local e_all e_code e_live
  e_all="$(  grep '^EDGE ' <<<"$dec" | sort -u)"
  e_code="$( grep '^EDGE ' <<<"$cod" | sort -u)"
  e_live="$( grep '^EDGE ' <<<"$ena" | sort -u)"
  local prose test_only
  prose="$(     comm -23 <(printf '%s\n' "$e_all")  <(printf '%s\n' "$e_code"))"
  test_only="$( comm -23 <(printf '%s\n' "$e_code") <(printf '%s\n' "$e_live"))"
  local n_all n_live n_prose n_test
  n_all=$(  grep -c . <<<"$e_all");     n_live=$( grep -c . <<<"$e_live")
  n_prose=$(grep -c . <<<"$prose");     n_test=$( grep -c . <<<"$test_only")
  local n_un=$(( n_prose + n_test )) pct=0
  [ "$n_all" -gt 0 ] && pct=$(( 100 * n_un / n_all ))

  cls_count() { grep -c " $2\$" <<<"$1"; }
  local dc ds dp du dl ec es ep eu el
  dc=$(cls_count "$dec" CORE); ds=$(cls_count "$dec" SOURCE); dp=$(cls_count "$dec" PERIPHERAL); du=$(cls_count "$dec" UNKNOWN)
  ec=$(cls_count "$ena" CORE); es=$(cls_count "$ena" SOURCE); ep=$(cls_count "$ena" PERIPHERAL); eu=$(cls_count "$ena" UNKNOWN)
  dl=$(grep -c '^LOOP ' <<<"$dec"); el=$(grep -c '^LOOP ' <<<"$ena")

  # CRON-CHAINED: a tool sharing a cron line with another mesh tool (`mesh-load-gate x 11 && mesh-y`)
  # is consumed by the CRON TABLE, which neither graph scans — its demotion is about scripts only.
  # Marked, not suppressed: an unmarked demotion is the strong claim, a marked one is a caveat.
  local chained
  chained="$(grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*#' "$CRON" 2>/dev/null | awk '
      { n = 0; delete seen; delete toks; s = $0
        while (match(s, /mesh-[a-z0-9-]+/)) { t = substr(s, RSTART, RLENGTH); s = substr(s, RSTART + RLENGTH)
          if (!(t in seen)) { seen[t] = 1; toks[++n] = t } }
        if (n > 1) for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) print toks[i] }' | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"

  # DEMOTED = a reflex whose class is weaker in the enacted graph than in the declared one.
  local demoted
  demoted="$(join <(grep -vE '^(LOOP|EDGE) ' <<<"$dec" | sort) <(grep -vE '^(LOOP|EDGE) ' <<<"$ena" | sort) \
             | awk -v CH=" $chained " '$2 != $3 {
                 mark = (index(CH, " " $1 " ") > 0) ? "   [cron-chained]" : ""
                 print "    - "$1"   "$2" -> "$3 mark }' | sort)"
  local n_dem n_chain; n_dem=$(grep -c . <<<"$demoted"); n_chain=$(grep -c 'cron-chained' <<<"$demoted")

  echo "mesh-closure --enacted: which enablement edges are actually EXERCISED"
  echo "  (Di Paolo/Buhrmann/Barandiaran's habit criterion — a support that does not depend on the"
  echo "   EXERCISE of the scheme is not a habit relation; Ramirez-Vizcaya, Synthese 206:137, 2025)"
  echo "  edges (both ends wired+present): declared=$n_all  LIVE=$n_live  PROSE-only=$n_prose  TEST-only=$n_test"
  echo "  UNEXERCISED=$n_un (${pct}% of the declared graph — closure held by text no run ever walks)"
  echo "  class shift (declared -> enacted):"
  echo "    CORE $dc -> $ec    SOURCE $ds -> $es    PERIPHERAL $dp -> $ep    UNKNOWN $du -> $eu"
  echo "    LOOPS $dl -> $el"
  if [ "$n_dem" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  DEMOTED=$n_dem (interior only by unexercised mentions — advisory, judge each);"
    echo "    $n_chain of them are [cron-chained]: they share a cron line with another mesh tool, so"
    echo "    their consumer may be that command chain, which NO axis of this tool scans."
    printf '%s\n' "$demoted"
  else
    echo "  DEMOTED=0 (every interior classification survives the exercise test)"
  fi
  if [ "$n_un" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  unexercised edges (producer -> consumer, site):"
    # awk truncates and ANNOUNCES the remainder (a silent top-N reads as "that is all of them"),
    # and no `head` in the pipe: pipefail + an early-exiting head turns a correct report into rc=141.
    { printf '%s\n' "$prose"     | awk 'NF { print $2, $3, "prose" }'
      printf '%s\n' "$test_only" | awk 'NF { print $2, $3, "test"  }'; } \
      | sort | awk -v N="${MESH_CLOSURE_ENACTED_TOP:-15}" '
          { n++; if (n <= N) printf "    - %s -> %s   [%s-only]\n", $1, $2, $3 }
          END { if (n > N) printf "    ... and %d more unexercised edge(s) (MESH_CLOSURE_ENACTED_TOP=%d)\n", n - N, N }'
  fi
}

# --- --null: does a closure number have a null it can FAIL against? ------------------------------
# Nave, "Beyond Control: Finding the Purpose of Enactive Cognitive Science", Adaptive Behavior,
# 9 Apr 2026, doi:10.1177/10597123261435796 (open access). Her charge against the cybernetic/DST
# branch of enactive cognitive science is not that its formalisms are wrong but that they are
# UNFALSIFIABLE: "any activity can be retroactively described as satisfying some underlying
# stability equation", so "the observation that we 'find' purposiveness in dynamical analyses
# reveals nothing about reality; it reflects only the formalism's expressive capacity." A closure
# statistic that no arrangement of the same parts could fail is a property of the measuring
# apparatus, not of the mesh. This axis supplies the missing null: a DEGREE-PRESERVING rewiring of
# the mesh's own enablement graph — same tools, same number of mentions each way per tool, every
# who-mentions-whom shuffled — and asks which headline numbers survive it.
#
# The answer is already visible in analyze(): a reflex's class is
#     PERIPHERAL  if nobody mentions it            (in-degree == 0)
#     CORE        else if it mentions somebody     (out-degree > 0)
#     SOURCE      else                             (in>0, out==0)
# — a function of TWO BITS of each node's own degree pair and of nothing else about the graph.
# A degree-preserving null holds both bits fixed at every node, so CORE / SOURCE / PERIPHERAL /
# UNKNOWN are invariant with EXACTLY ZERO variance: they are a restatement of the degree sequence
# and cannot discriminate the mesh from a randomly rewired mesh. Only LOOPS (mutual a<->b pairs)
# is a second-order statistic the null can move, so it is the only one this axis tests.
#
# Rewiring the wired+present SUBGRAPH is sufficient and exact: full-graph degree = subgraph degree
# + edges to non-wired/absent tokens, which the swap never touches — so every node's full in/out
# degree, and hence its reported class, is preserved. No caveat is owed on that leg.
#
# HONESTY BOUNDS
#   - The null is a configuration model: it preserves degrees and destroys everything else
#     (reciprocity, clustering, the sense->fusion layering). Beating it is evidence of SOME
#     structure beyond degree, never evidence that the structure is the one we tell ourselves.
#   - It inherits every bound of the graph it is handed. On the declared population the edges are
#     mostly prose (--enacted: ~75%), so a significant declared LOOPS excess is significance about
#     how the mesh WRITES, not how it RUNS. The live population is the one to read.
#   - p is the add-one-corrected empirical tail, so the floor is 1/(R+1); with the default R=200 no
#     result can be reported below p=0.005 and "p=0.005" means "no replicate reached it", not zero.
#   - Deterministic: seeded (MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SEED). Two runs on the same graph agree exactly; a
#     number that moves between runs on an unchanged genome is a bug, not sampling.
#   - The accepted-swap count per replicate is RANDOMISED, not fixed. A fixed count locks the
#     chain's parity on a degree-regular graph and can pin the null exactly on the observation
#     (see the PARITY TRAP note in the program below) — a null that agrees with you for an
#     algebraic reason is the worst possible null, because it reads as a confident WITHIN NULL.
render_null() {
  local dec ena
  dec="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON" 1 all)"  || return $?
  ena="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON" 1 live)" || return $?

  local cls_dec cls_ena
  cls_dec="$(printf '%s %s %s %s' \
      "$(grep -c ' CORE$' <<<"$dec")" "$(grep -c ' SOURCE$' <<<"$dec")" \
      "$(grep -c ' PERIPHERAL$' <<<"$dec")" "$(grep -c ' UNKNOWN$' <<<"$dec")")"
  cls_ena="$(printf '%s %s %s %s' \
      "$(grep -c ' CORE$' <<<"$ena")" "$(grep -c ' SOURCE$' <<<"$ena")" \
      "$(grep -c ' PERIPHERAL$' <<<"$ena")" "$(grep -c ' UNKNOWN$' <<<"$ena")")"

  local R="${MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_R:-200}" SEED="${MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SEED:-20260818}"
  local SW="${MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SWAPS:-10}" TMO="${MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_TIMEOUT:-180}"

  echo "mesh-closure --null: which closure numbers can FAIL a degree-preserving null?"
  echo "  (Nave, Adaptive Behavior 2026 (doi:10.1177/10597123261435796) — a formalism that finds"
  echo "   purpose in every arrangement reports its own expressive capacity, not the system.)"
  echo "  null = configuration model: same tools, same in/out mention-degree per tool, edges reshuffled"
  echo "  R=$R replicates  seed=$SEED  swaps/replicate=${SW}xE"

  # the null program goes to a FILE, never `python3 - <<EOF`: a heredoc IS stdin, so a heredoc
  # program and a piped payload cannot coexist — the heredoc silently eats the edge list.
  local nd; nd="$(mktemp -d)"; trap 'rm -rf "$nd"' RETURN
  cat > "$nd/null.py" <<'PYEOF'
import sys, random
pop, R, seed, swmul = sys.argv[1], int(sys.argv[2]), int(sys.argv[3]), int(sys.argv[4])
edges = []
for ln in sys.stdin:
    p = ln.split()
    if len(p) == 2:
        edges.append((p[0], p[1]))
edges = sorted(set(edges))
E = len(edges)
nodes = sorted({u for u, v in edges} | {v for u, v in edges})
def mutual(es):
    s = set(es)
    return sum(1 for (u, v) in s if u < v and (v, u) in s)
obs = mutual(edges)
if E < 4:
    print("  %-8s edges=%d  LOOPS observed=%d  null=n/a (fewer than 4 edges to swap)" % (pop, E, obs))
    sys.exit(0)
rng = random.Random(seed)
# degree-preserving directed double-edge swap: (a->b),(c->d) => (a->d),(c->b).
# Out-degree of a and c and in-degree of b and d are all unchanged by construction.
nswap = swmul * E
counts = []
for rep in range(R):
    es = list(edges)
    S = set(es)
    # PARITY TRAP (found on this tool's own 4-node fixture, 2026-08-18): with a FIXED number of
    # accepted swaps the chain cannot leave its coset on a degree-regular graph. Where every node
    # has in=out=1 the graph IS a permutation and one accepted swap is exactly one transposition,
    # so an even swap count can only ever reach even permutations. On n1<->n2, n3<->n4 that meant
    # every one of 50 replicates came back with mutual=2: "null mean=2.00 sd=0.00", a null
    # perfectly equal to the observation, reported with total confidence. Draw the target from the
    # seeded rng instead — still reproducible, no fixed parity.
    target = nswap + rng.randrange(nswap)
    done = 0
    tries = 0
    lim = target * 20
    while done < target and tries < lim:
        tries += 1
        i = rng.randrange(E); j = rng.randrange(E)
        if i == j:
            continue
        a, b = es[i]; c, d = es[j]
        if a == d or c == b:
            continue
        if (a, d) in S or (c, b) in S:
            continue
        S.discard((a, b)); S.discard((c, d))
        S.add((a, d)); S.add((c, b))
        es[i] = (a, d); es[j] = (c, b)
        done += 1
    counts.append(mutual(es))
m = sum(counts) / float(R)
var = sum((c - m) ** 2 for c in counts) / float(R)
sd = var ** 0.5
ge = sum(1 for c in counts if c >= obs)
p = (1 + ge) / float(R + 1)
z = (obs - m) / sd if sd > 0 else float('inf') if obs != m else 0.0
zs = ("%.2f" % z) if z != float('inf') else "inf"
verdict = "ABOVE NULL" if p < 0.05 else "WITHIN NULL — no evidence of reciprocity beyond degree"
print("  %-8s edges=%-4d nodes=%-4d  LOOPS observed=%-4d  null mean=%.2f sd=%.2f  z=%s  p=%.3f  %s"
      % (pop, E, len(nodes), obs, m, sd, zs, p, verdict))
PYEOF

  local pop
  for pop in declared live; do
    local src edges
    case "$pop" in declared) src="$dec" ;; live) src="$ena" ;; esac
    edges="$(grep '^EDGE ' <<<"$src" | awk '{print $2, $3}' | sort -u)"
    printf '%s\n' "$edges" | timeout "$TMO" python3 "$nd/null.py" "$pop" "$R" "$SEED" "$SW" || return 1
  done

  echo "  CORE / SOURCE / PERIPHERAL / UNKNOWN: NULL-INVARIANT BY CONSTRUCTION, variance exactly 0."
  echo "    analyze() derives a class from two bits — (does anyone mention me?) x (do I mention"
  echo "    anyone?) — which a degree-preserving null holds fixed at every node. So"
  echo "      declared $cls_dec  and  live $cls_ena   (CORE SOURCE PERIPHERAL UNKNOWN)"
  echo "    are reproduced EXACTLY by a mesh whose every dependency has been reshuffled at random."
  echo "    They are degree bookkeeping, not evidence of organization. That is a property of"
  echo "    analyze()'s rule, and it is measured, not asserted: --test drives two fixtures with one"
  echo "    degree sequence and different wirings (n1<->n2,n3<->n4 vs the 4-cycle) through the real"
  echo "    classifier and requires identical class counts with LOOPS 2 vs 0, against a third"
  echo "    fixture at a different degree sequence that must NOT match. LOOPS is the only line"
  echo "    above that can fail a null."
}

# --- --symmetry: the DYNAMICAL closure axis (structure <-> activity directional coupling) ------
SYM_REPO="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_REPO:-$(dirname "$SCRIPTS")}"
SYM_BIN_H="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_BIN_H:-6}"
SYM_TAU_H="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_TAU_H:-48}"
SYM_DAYS="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_DAYS:-70}"
SYM_LMAX="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_LMAX:-4}"
SYM_ALPHA="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_ALPHA:-0.05}"
SYM_MIN_N="${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_MIN_N:-40}"

# raw churn stream: "C <epoch>" commit headers interleaved with tab-separated numstat rows.
sym_raw() {
  if [ -n "${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG:-}" ]; then
    [ -r "$MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG" ] || {
      echo "mesh-closure: SYM_GITLOG fixture unreadable: $MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG" >&2; return 3; }
    cat "$MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG"; return 0
  fi
  [ -d "$SYM_REPO/.git" ] || {
    echo "mesh-closure: --symmetry needs the genome's own git history; no repo at $SYM_REPO" >&2; return 3; }
  git -C "$SYM_REPO" log --since="${SYM_DAYS}.days" --pretty=format:'C %at' --numstat 2>/dev/null
}

# "<bin> <gamma> <activity>" per bin. gamma = mean edit-persistence at horizon tau, PUBLISHED at
# the bin where that horizon closes (the leakage guard); activity = log1p(lines changed).
sym_series() {
  sym_raw | awk -F'\t' '
    /^C [0-9]+$/ { split($0, c, " "); ts = c[2] + 0; next }
    NF >= 3 { a = $1; d = $2; if (a == "-") { a = 0; d = 0 }      # binary diffs carry no line count
              printf "%d\t%d\t%d\t%s\n", ts, a + 0, d + 0, $3 }
  ' | sort -k1,1n -s | awk -F'\t' -v BINH="$SYM_BIN_H" -v TAUH="$SYM_TAU_H" '
    # a rename row is "pre/{old => new}/post" or "old => new"; resolve both ends
    function side(p, which,   pre, post, inner, q) {
      if (index(p, "=>") == 0) return p
      if (match(p, /\{[^{}]*=>[^{}]*\}/)) {
        pre = substr(p, 1, RSTART - 1); post = substr(p, RSTART + RLENGTH)
        inner = substr(p, RSTART + 1, RLENGTH - 2)
        split(inner, q, "=>"); gsub(/^ +| +$/, "", q[which]); return pre q[which] post }
      split(p, q, "=>"); gsub(/^ +| +$/, "", q[which]); return q[which] }
    function res(p,   i) { for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) { if (p in alias) p = alias[p]; else break } return p }
    { n++; ts[n] = $1; ch[n] = $2 + $3
      if (index($4, "=>")) alias[side($4, 1)] = side($4, 2)
      key[n] = side($4, 2) }
    END {
      if (n == 0) { print "ERR nodata"; exit }
      tau = TAUH * 3600; binsec = BINH * 3600; shift = int(TAUH / BINH + 0.5)
      t0 = ts[1]; last = ts[n]
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { k = res(key[i]); key[i] = k; cnt[k]++; ev[k "," cnt[k]] = i; pos[i] = cnt[k] }
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        k = key[i]; horizon = ts[i] + tau
        if (horizon > last) continue                    # horizon past the record -> unassessable
        s = 1; j = pos[i] + 1
        while (j <= cnt[k]) { e = ev[k "," j]
          if (ts[e] > horizon) break
          if (ch[e] > 0) { s = 0; break }               # rewritten inside tau
          s = 0.8; j++ }                                # pure relocation inside tau (lambda)
        b = int((ts[i] - t0) / binsec) + shift          # LEAKAGE GUARD: publish at horizon close
        gs[b] += s; gn[b]++ }
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { b = int((ts[i] - t0) / binsec); act[b] += ch[i] }
      bmax = int((last - t0) / binsec)
      g = -1
      for (b = 0; b <= bmax; b++) {
        if (gn[b] > 0) g = gs[b] / gn[b]
        if (g < 0) continue                             # no persistence verdict is knowable yet
        printf "%d %.6f %.6f\n", b, g, log(1 + (act[b] + 0)) }
    }'
}

# estimator: reads "<bin> <gamma> <activity>", emits one line
#   "<n> <L> <deseasoned> <dS> <dA> <g_sa> <p_sa> <g_as> <p_as> <ratio> <VERDICT>"
sym_gc() {
  awk -v LMAX="$SYM_LMAX" -v BINH="$SYM_BIN_H" -v ALPHA="$SYM_ALPHA" -v MINN="$SYM_MIN_N" '
    # OLS residual sum of squares for Y ~ D (k columns, intercept included); -1 if singular
    function rss(Y, D, m, k,   i, j, t, A, b, c, r, mx, mi, tmp, v, f, sum, beta, e, q) {
      for (i = 1; i <= k; i++) { b[i] = 0; for (j = 1; j <= k; j++) A[i "," j] = 0 }
      for (t = 1; t <= m; t++) for (i = 1; i <= k; i++) { b[i] += D[t "," i] * Y[t]
          for (j = i; j <= k; j++) A[i "," j] += D[t "," i] * D[t "," j] }
      for (i = 1; i <= k; i++) for (j = 1; j < i; j++) A[i "," j] = A[j "," i]
      for (c = 1; c <= k; c++) {                                   # gaussian elim, partial pivot
        mx = -1; mi = c
        for (r = c; r <= k; r++) { v = A[r "," c]; if (v < 0) v = -v; if (v > mx) { mx = v; mi = r } }
        if (mx < 1e-10) return -1
        if (mi != c) { for (j = 1; j <= k; j++) { tmp = A[c "," j]; A[c "," j] = A[mi "," j]; A[mi "," j] = tmp }
                       tmp = b[c]; b[c] = b[mi]; b[mi] = tmp }
        for (r = c + 1; r <= k; r++) { f = A[r "," c] / A[c "," c]; if (f == 0) continue
          for (j = c; j <= k; j++) A[r "," j] -= f * A[c "," j]
          b[r] -= f * b[c] } }
      for (i = k; i >= 1; i--) { sum = b[i]; for (j = i + 1; j <= k; j++) sum -= A[i "," j] * beta[j]
        beta[i] = sum / A[i "," i] }
      q = 0
      for (t = 1; t <= m; t++) { e = Y[t]; for (j = 1; j <= k; j++) e -= D[t "," j] * beta[j]; q += e * e }
      return q }
    # Granger statistic ln(RSS_restricted/RSS_unrestricted) for X -> Y at lag L
    function gcv(Y, X, n, L, start,   t, m, i, DR, DU, YY, rr, ru) {
      m = 0
      for (t = start; t <= n; t++) { m++; YY[m] = Y[t]; DR[m "," 1] = 1; DU[m "," 1] = 1
        for (i = 1; i <= L; i++) { DR[m "," (1 + i)] = Y[t - i]
          DU[m "," (1 + i)] = Y[t - i]; DU[m "," (1 + L + i)] = X[t - i] } }
      rr = rss(YY, DR, m, 1 + L); ru = rss(YY, DU, m, 1 + 2 * L)
      if (rr <= 0 || ru <= 0) return -1
      return log(rr / ru) }
    function aicv(Y, X, n, L, start,   t, m, i, DU, YY, ru, k) {
      m = 0
      for (t = start; t <= n; t++) { m++; YY[m] = Y[t]; DU[m "," 1] = 1
        for (i = 1; i <= L; i++) { DU[m "," (1 + i)] = Y[t - i]; DU[m "," (1 + L + i)] = X[t - i] } }
      k = 1 + 2 * L; ru = rss(YY, DU, m, k)
      if (ru <= 0) return 1e18
      return m * log(ru / m) + 2 * k }
    function unitroot(Z, n,   i, mu, num, den) {
      mu = 0; for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) mu += Z[i]; mu /= n
      num = 0; den = 0
      for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) num += (Z[i] - mu) * (Z[i - 1] - mu)
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) den += (Z[i] - mu) ^ 2
      if (den <= 0) return 0
      return (num / den >= 0.9) ? 1 : 0 }
    # EXACT circular-shift permutation p-value on the SOURCE series (autocorrelation-preserving)
    function pval(Y, X, n, L, start, obs,   k, i, XS, g, ge, tot) {
      if (obs < 0) return -1
      ge = 0; tot = 0
      for (k = 1; k < n; k++) {
        for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) XS[i] = X[((i - 1 + k) % n) + 1]
        g = gcv(Y, XS, n, L, start); if (g < 0) continue
        tot++; if (g >= obs) ge++ }
      if (tot == 0) return -1
      return (1 + ge) / (1 + tot) }
    /^ERR/ { err = 1; next }
    NF >= 3 { n++; S[n] = $2 + 0; A[n] = $3 + 0 }
    END {
      if (err || n < MINN) { printf "%d 0 0 0 0 -1 -1 -1 -1 -1 UNDETERMINED\n", n + 0; exit }
      P = int(24 / BINH + 0.0001); if (P < 1) P = 1
      if (P > 1 && n >= 2 * P) {                                   # strip the diurnal common drive
        for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { ph = (i - 1) % P; ms[ph] += S[i]; ma[ph] += A[i]; mc[ph]++ }
        for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { ph = (i - 1) % P; S[i] -= ms[ph] / mc[ph]; A[i] -= ma[ph] / mc[ph] }
        des = 1 }
      ds = unitroot(S, n); da = unitroot(A, n)
      if (ds) for (i = 1; i < n; i++) S[i] = S[i + 1] - S[i]
      if (da) for (i = 1; i < n; i++) A[i] = A[i + 1] - A[i]
      if (ds || da) n--
      start = LMAX + 1; bestL = 1; best = 1e19                     # AIC over a FIXED sample
      for (L = 1; L <= LMAX; L++) { v = aicv(A, S, n, L, start) + aicv(S, A, n, L, start)
        if (v < best) { best = v; bestL = L } }
      L = bestL
      gsa = gcv(A, S, n, L, start); gas = gcv(S, A, n, L, start)
      psa = pval(A, S, n, L, start, gsa); pas = pval(S, A, n, L, start, gas)
      if (gsa < 0 || gas < 0 || psa < 0 || pas < 0) {
        printf "%d %d %d %d %d -1 -1 -1 -1 -1 UNDETERMINED\n", n, L, des + 0, ds, da; exit }
      sigsa = (psa < ALPHA); sigas = (pas < ALPHA)
      if      (sigsa && sigas) verdict = "SYMMETRIZED"
      else if (sigas)          verdict = "ACTIVITY-DRIVEN"
      else if (sigsa)          verdict = "STRUCTURE-DRIVEN"
      else                     verdict = "DECOUPLED"
      ratio = (gas > 0 ? gsa / gas : -1)
      printf "%d %d %d %d %d %.6f %.4f %.6f %.4f %.3f %s\n", n, L, des + 0, ds, da, gsa, psa, gas, pas, ratio, verdict }
  '
}

render_symmetry() {
  local series
  if [ -n "${MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES:-}" ]; then
    [ -r "$MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES" ] || {
      echo "mesh-closure: SYM_SERIES fixture unreadable: $MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES" >&2; return 3; }
    series="$(cat "$MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES")"
  else
    series="$(sym_series)" || return $?
  fi
  local n L des ds da gsa psa gas pas ratio verdict
  read -r n L des ds da gsa psa gas pas ratio verdict < <(sym_gc <<<"$series")

  echo "mesh-closure --symmetry: causal SYMMETRIZATION of structure <-> activity"
  echo "  (Gosme, arXiv:2512.09352 — reciprocal constraint is the dynamical signature of closure)"
  echo "  series: STRUCTURE = Gamma, persistence of an edit at tau=${SYM_TAU_H}h (published at horizon close)"
  echo "          ACTIVITY  = log1p(lines changed); ${SYM_BIN_H}h bins over ${SYM_DAYS}d of genome history"
  if [ "$verdict" = "UNDETERMINED" ]; then
    echo "  usable bins n=$n (need >= $SYM_MIN_N) -> UNDETERMINED (honest n/a: too little history or a"
    echo "  degenerate regression — no verdict is manufactured from it)"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "  n=$n bins  VAR lag L=$L (AIC)  deseasoned=$des  differenced: S=$ds A=$da"
  echo "  g(structure->activity) = $gsa   p=$psa   (exact circular-shift null, $((n-1)) shifts)"
  echo "  g(activity->structure) = $gas   p=$pas"
  echo "  symmetry ratio (S->A)/(A->S) = $ratio   [continuous index; the label below is significance-"
  echo "                                            based, NOT the paper's 0.71/0.94 cut-points]"
  echo "  VERDICT: $verdict"
  case "$verdict" in
    SYMMETRIZED)      echo "    both directions beat their own null — reciprocal constraint between the" ;;
    ACTIVITY-DRIVEN)  echo "    the constraint network FOLLOWS the work and does not measurably shape it:" ;;
    STRUCTURE-DRIVEN) echo "    work follows the network, but the network gets no feedback from the work:" ;;
    DECOUPLED)        echo "    neither direction beats its null — structure and activity are two" ;;
  esac
  case "$verdict" in
    SYMMETRIZED)      echo "    constraint network and the activity it organizes. Static closure is matched" ;
                      echo "    by dynamical closure." ;;
    ACTIVITY-DRIVEN)  echo "    structural closure (see the graph above) WITHOUT operational closure. This is" ;
                      echo "    the paper's exploratory regime, and the dynamical twin of the allopoiesis gap." ;;
    STRUCTURE-DRIVEN) echo "    half of the reciprocal relation is missing — the network is not repaired by" ;
                      echo "    what it produces." ;;
    DECOUPLED)        echo "    processes sharing a repository, coupled only through whoever runs them." ;;
  esac
}

# --- --semantic: SEMANTIC closure — the PROVENANCE of every decision threshold ------------------
SEM_TOP="${MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TOP:-12}"
SEM_TRIVIAL="${MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TRIVIAL-0 1}"

# Emits one "<tool> <CLASS> <operand>" line per decision site, plus "<tool> OPAQUE -" for a tool with
# no traceable non-trivial site whose interpreter lives inside an embedded awk/python/jq body.
# Two passes over each file: pass 1 learns where every variable's value comes from, pass 2 reads the
# comparisons. Bash assigns before it compares, so one look-ahead pass is enough.
sem_sites() {
  local scripts="${1:-$SCRIPTS}" f
  [ -d "$scripts" ] || { echo "mesh-closure: scripts dir not found: $scripts" >&2; return 3; }
  for f in "$scripts"/mesh-*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    awk -v TOOL="$(basename "$f")" -v TRIV="$SEM_TRIVIAL" '
      function rank(k) { return (k == "SELF" ? 3 : (k == "UNRESOLVED" ? 2 : 1)) }
      # does this text READ one of the mesh own records?
      function reads_record(s) {
        return (s ~ /mesh-[a-z0-9-]+/ || s ~ /[.]mesh\// || s ~ /[.]log/ ||
                s ~ /[.]tsv/ || s ~ /[.]jsonl/ || s ~ /[.]csv/)
      }
      # ...and is that "read" only a lookup of an AUTHORED constant? The threshold-ledger holds the
      # mesh scattered magic numbers as one data file, resolved via `mesh-sexpr-gate --const`. That
      # is a real engineering good (recalibrate by a data diff, not a script edit) and it is NOT
      # semantic closure: nobody in the organization PRODUCED the 90, it just moved from a script
      # line to a table row. Checked BEFORE reads_record, or the ledger hop would mint SELF for
      # every one of the 20 tools that consume it and the metric would measure indirection.
      function const_lookup(s) {
        return (s ~ /--const/ && (s ~ /sexpr-gate/ || s ~ /threshold-ledger/)) ||
               (s ~ /_ledger_const/)
      }
      # provenance of a FUNCTION defined in this same file (pass 1 collected the bodies).
      # The read is usually one hop away from the assignment: the site says $(helper ...) and the
      # helper body is what touches the record. Memoized, and seeded UNRESOLVED before recursing so
      # a mutually-recursive pair terminates at "cannot trace" instead of looping.
      function fnclass(nm,   b) {
        if (nm in fncls) return fncls[nm]
        if (!(nm in fnbody)) return ""
        fncls[nm] = "UNRESOLVED"
        b = fnbody[nm]
        fncls[nm] = const_lookup(b) ? "IMPOSED" : (reads_record(b) ? "SELF" : callclass(b))
        if (fncls[nm] == "") fncls[nm] = "UNRESOLVED"
        return fncls[nm]
      }
      # best provenance over every $(name ...) in a text whose name is a function of this file
      function callclass(s,   best, k, nm, t) {
        best = ""; t = s
        while (match(t, /\$\([ \t]*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*/)) {
          nm = substr(t, RSTART + 2, RLENGTH - 2); gsub(/^[ \t]+/, "", nm)
          t = substr(t, RSTART + RLENGTH)
          k = fnclass(nm)
          if (k != "" && (best == "" || rank(k) > rank(best))) best = k
        }
        return best
      }
      # provenance of an assignment RHS
      function kindof(rhs,   t, k, nm) {
        # a pure $(( ... )) arithmetic expansion carries no command substitution: not traceable
        if (rhs ~ /\$\(\(/ && rhs !~ /\$\([^(]/) return "UNRESOLVED"
        if (rhs ~ /\$\(/ || rhs ~ /<\(/) {
          if (const_lookup(rhs)) return "IMPOSED"
          if (reads_record(rhs)) return "SELF"
          k = callclass(rhs)                       # one hop through this file own helpers
          if (k != "") return k
          return "UNRESOLVED"
        }
        t = rhs
        sub(/[ \t]*#.*$/, "", t)
        gsub(/\047/, "", t); gsub(/"/, "", t); gsub(/[ \t]/, "", t)
        # NOT special-cased: `X="${X:-30}"` where the guarded name IS the assigned one. It reads as a
        # nullity guard over a runtime value AND as the canonical env-override-with-a-literal-default,
        # which this axis defines as IMPOSED — and no fixture separates the two. The `read` leg below
        # already resolves the case that matters (a value that entered the name by a runtime read),
        # so the ambiguous rule is left out rather than asserted.
        if (t ~ /^\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:-[0-9]+([.][0-9]+)?\}$/) return "IMPOSED"
        if (t ~ /^[0-9]+([.][0-9]+)?$/) return "IMPOSED"
        # a bare alias  X="$Y" / X="${Y}"  carries Y provenance, it does not erase it
        if (t ~ /^\$\{?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\}?$/) {
          nm = t; gsub(/[\$\{\}]/, "", nm)
          return varclass(nm)
        }
        return "UNRESOLVED"
      }
      function varclass(nm) { return (nm in org) ? org[nm] : "UNRESOLVED" }
      # class of a compared-against operand; "" means "not a numeric decision site"
      function opclass(tok,   nm) {
        gsub(/\047/, "", tok); gsub(/"/, "", tok)
        if (tok ~ /^[0-9]+([.][0-9]+)?$/) return ((" " TRIV " ") ~ (" " tok " ") ? "TRIVIAL" : "IMPOSED")
        if (tok ~ /^\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:-[0-9]+([.][0-9]+)?\}$/) {
          nm = tok; sub(/^.*:-/, "", nm); sub(/\}$/, "", nm)
          return ((" " TRIV " ") ~ (" " nm " ") ? "TRIVIAL" : "IMPOSED")
        }
        if (tok ~ /^\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\}$/) { nm = substr(tok, 3, length(tok) - 3); return varclass(nm) }
        if (tok ~ /^\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/)     { nm = substr(tok, 2);                  return varclass(nm) }
        return ""
      }
      function emit(c, seg) { if (c == "") return; print TOOL, c, seg; if (c != "TRIVIAL") nsite++ }
      # a test-harness function body is not the organ: its literals are fixture data, not
      # interpreters. Same exact-component rule --enacted uses (parse_latest must stay live), and
      # it is what stops THIS tool from counting its own --test fixture strings as decision sites.
      function is_test_fn(nm,   p, i, k) {
        k = split(nm, p, "_")
        for (i = 1; i <= k; i++)
          if (p[i] == "test" || p[i] == "tests" || p[i] == "testing" || p[i] == "smoke" || p[i] == "selftest")
            return 1
        return (nm ~ /selftest$/)
      }
      function fnname(line,   nm) { nm = line; sub(/[ \t]*\(\).*/, "", nm); gsub(/^[ \t]+/, "", nm); return nm }
      # a one-line function is one whose braces BALANCE on the opener — not one that ENDS in `}`.
      # `rank(){ case ... esac; }  # 0 = UNKNOWN` ends in a comment, so the ends-in-} rule read it as
      # multi-line and swallowed the NEXT function whole; mesh-situation rank() inherited
      # situation_decide() body and reported SELF off a borrowed record read.
      function balanced(line,   t, o, c) {
        t = line; o = gsub(/\{/, "{", t); t = line; c = gsub(/\}/, "}", t)
        return (o > 0 && o == c)
      }
      FNR == 1 { pass++ }
      # ---- pass 1: the body of every function defined in this file ----
      # Same column-0 opener + brace-DEPTH convention the test-body skip uses, so a heredoc that
      # contains its own `name() {` ... `}` cannot truncate the body it sits in. A body is collected
      # for its PROVENANCE only; the sites inside it are still read (or skipped) by pass 3.
      pass == 1 {
        if ($0 ~ /^[ \t]*#/) next
        if (!infn) {
          if (match($0, /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*[ \t]*\([ \t]*\)[ \t]*\{/)) {
            curfn = $0; sub(/[ \t]*\(.*$/, "", curfn)
            fnbody[curfn] = fnbody[curfn] " " substr($0, RSTART + RLENGTH)
            if (balanced($0)) curfn = ""          # one-liner: the opener closes it
            else { infn = 1; d0 = 1 }
          }
          next
        }
        if ($0 ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*[ \t]*\([ \t]*\)[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/) d0++
        else if ($0 ~ /^\}[ \t]*$/) { d0--; if (d0 <= 0) { infn = 0; curfn = ""; next } }
        fnbody[curfn] = fnbody[curfn] " " $0
        next
      }
      # ---- pass 2: provenance of every assigned variable ----
      pass == 2 {
        if ($0 ~ /^[ \t]*#/) next
        if (!intest1 && $0 ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*\(\)[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/ && is_test_fn(fnname($0))) { intest1 = 1; d1 = 1; next }
        if (intest1) {
          # brace DEPTH, not the first `}`: a harness commonly writes fixture FILES whose text
          # contains a column-0 `name() {` ... `}` of its own, and a depth-blind skip would end
          # there and let the fixture literals into the census. The --test of THIS tool writes
          # exactly such a fixture, which is how the leak was found.
          if ($0 ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*\(\)[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/) d1++
          else if ($0 ~ /^\}[ \t]*$/) { d1--; if (d1 <= 0) intest1 = 0 }
          next
        }
        l = $0
        sub(/^[ \t]+/, "", l)
        # `A="$B"; C=absolute` is TWO assignments, and reading it as one made the RHS unparseable —
        # which is how the alias hop was lost on exactly the lines that carry it (a band lowered
        # inside `{ X="$rel"; SRC=p50; }`). Only split when no substitution can be cut in half.
        nseg = (l ~ /\$\(/ || l ~ /<\(/ || l ~ /\(\(/) ? 1 : split(l, segs, /;/)
        if (nseg == 1) segs[1] = l
        for (si = 1; si <= nseg; si++) {
          sl = segs[si]
          gsub(/^[ \t{}]+/, "", sl)
          sub(/^(local|declare|export|readonly|typeset)[ \t]+(-[A-Za-z]+[ \t]+)?/, "", sl)
          # `read -r a b <<<"$out"` IS an assignment, and it is how a runtime value enters a name.
          # Invisible, it lets a later `a="${a:-0}"` nullity guard stand as the whole provenance and
          # a measured quantity is filed as a freestanding constant. Marked UNRESOLVED, not SELF:
          # what the read consumed is a separate question this scan does not follow.
          if (match(sl, /^read[ \t]+/)) {
            rest = substr(sl, RLENGTH + 1)
            while (match(rest, /^-[A-Za-z]+[ \t]*/)) rest = substr(rest, RLENGTH + 1)
            nr = split(rest, rv, /[ \t]+/)
            for (ri = 1; ri <= nr; ri++) {
              if (rv[ri] !~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/) break
              if (!(rv[ri] in org) || rank("UNRESOLVED") > rank(org[rv[ri]])) org[rv[ri]] = "UNRESOLVED"
            }
          }
          if (match(sl, /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=/)) {
            v = substr(sl, 1, RLENGTH - 1); rhs = substr(sl, RLENGTH + 1)
            k = kindof(rhs)
            if (!(v in org) || rank(k) > rank(org[v])) org[v] = k
          }
        }
        next
      }
      # ---- pass 3: the decision sites ----
      pass == 3 {
        if ($0 ~ /^[ \t]*#/) next                       # a full-line comment is prose, never a site
        if (!intest2 && $0 ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*\(\)[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/ && is_test_fn(fnname($0))) { intest2 = 1; d2 = 1; next }
        if (intest2) {
          if ($0 ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*\(\)[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/) d2++
          else if ($0 ~ /^\}[ \t]*$/) { d2--; if (d2 <= 0) intest2 = 0 }
          next
        }
        if ($0 ~ /awk|python3?|jq / ) embedded = 1
        s = $0
        while (match(s, /[ \t](-gt|-ge|-lt|-le)[ \t]+[^ \t;)&|]+/)) {
          seg = substr(s, RSTART, RLENGTH); s = substr(s, RSTART + RLENGTH)
          sub(/^[ \t]*-(gt|ge|lt|le)[ \t]+/, "", seg)
          emit(opclass(seg), seg)
        }
        if (match($0, /\(\(.*\)\)/)) {
          a = substr($0, RSTART + 2, RLENGTH - 4)
          while (match(a, /(<|>|<=|>=)[ \t]*[^ \t;)&|<>]+/)) {
            seg = substr(a, RSTART, RLENGTH); a = substr(a, RSTART + RLENGTH)
            sub(/^[<>]=?[ \t]*/, "", seg)
            emit(opclass(seg), seg)
          }
        }
      }
      END { if (nsite + 0 == 0 && embedded) print TOOL, "OPAQUE", "-" }
    ' "$f" "$f" "$f"
  done
}

render_semantic() {
  local sites; sites="$(sem_sites "$SCRIPTS")" || return $?
  local out
  out="$(awk -v N="$SEM_TOP" '
    { cls[$2]++; per[$1 "|" $2]++; seen[$1] = 1 }
    END {
      self = cls["SELF"] + 0; imp = cls["IMPOSED"] + 0
      printf "SUM %d %d %d %d %d %d\n", self, imp, cls["UNRESOLVED"] + 0, cls["TRIVIAL"] + 0,
             cls["OPAQUE"] + 0, length(seen)
      for (k in per) {
        split(k, p, "|")
        if (p[2] == "IMPOSED") printf "DEBT %d %s %d %d\n", per[k], p[1],
                                      per[p[1] "|SELF"] + 0, per[p[1] "|UNRESOLVED"] + 0
        if (p[2] == "SELF" && per[p[1] "|IMPOSED"] + 0 == 0) printf "CLOSEDTOOL %s %d\n", p[1], per[k]
        if (p[2] == "OPAQUE") printf "OPAQUETOOL %s\n", p[1]
      }
    }' <<<"$sites")"

  local self imp unres triv opaque withsites
  read -r _ self imp unres triv opaque withsites < <(grep '^SUM ' <<<"$out")
  local tools=0 f
  for f in "$SCRIPTS"/mesh-*; do [ -f "$f" ] && tools=$(( tools + 1 )); done
  local assessable=$(( self + imp )) sci="n/a" rung="n/a"
  if [ "$assessable" -gt 0 ]; then
    sci="$(awk -v s="$self" -v a="$assessable" 'BEGIN { printf "%.4f", s / a }')"
    if   [ "$self" -eq 0 ];  then rung="IMPOSED  (rung 1 — every shell-visible interpreter is freestanding)"
    elif [ "$imp"  -eq 0 ];  then rung="CLOSED   (rung 4 — every traceable interpreter is self-manufactured)"
    else                          rung="PARTIAL  (between the rungs — some interpreters self-manufactured)"
    fi
  fi

  echo "mesh-closure --semantic: SEMANTIC closure — who wrote each sense's decision threshold"
  echo "  (Lopez-Diaz & Gershenson, J.R.Soc.Interface 22:20250784 (2025) / arXiv:2404.04374 — a system"
  echo "   is semantically closed only when the INTERPRETERS mapping its signals to functional"
  echo "   consequence are manufactured by the organization itself; a freestanding interpreter leaves"
  echo "   it on the lower rungs, robust only while something outside keeps supplying the constant.)"
  echo "  tools scanned=$tools ($withsites with a site or an opaque body)"
  echo "  decision sites: SELF=$self  IMPOSED=$imp  UNRESOLVED=$unres (honest n/a — operand untraceable)"
  echo "  TRIVIAL=$triv excluded (cut-point 0 or 1 — a presence test has no calibratable degree of freedom)"
  echo "  SCI = SELF/(SELF+IMPOSED) = $sci   over $assessable assessable sites   -> rung: $rung"
  echo "  OPAQUE=$opaque tools carry no traceable shell site but an embedded awk/python/jq interpreter"
  echo "                 body — their thresholds are invisible here, so SCI UNDER-counts SELF."
  echo "  provenance is not appropriateness: a literal can be exactly right. Report-only."
  if [ "$self" -gt 0 ]; then
    # SELF is the small side by construction, so print ALL of it rather than a count: a bare
    # "SELF=3" cannot show that a self-derived cut-point sits inside a SMOKE-TEST body, where it is
    # a gate assertion and not a sense's interpreter at all. The reader judges each.
    echo "  the self-derived interpreters, in full (judge each — a cut-point inside a --test body is"
    echo "  a gate assertion, not an organ's interpreter):"
    grep ' SELF ' <<<"$sites" | awk '{ printf "    * %-24s operand %s\n", $1, $3 }'
  fi
  if [ "$imp" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  deepest interpretive debt (most freestanding cut-points — where recalibration would pay):"
    # awk truncates and ANNOUNCES the remainder; no `head` in the pipe (pipefail + SIGPIPE = rc=141).
    grep '^DEBT ' <<<"$out" | sort -k2,2rn -k3,3 \
      | awk -v N="$SEM_TOP" '
          { n++; if (n <= N) printf "    - %-24s IMPOSED=%-4d SELF=%-3d UNRESOLVED=%d\n", $3, $2, $4, $5 }
          END { if (n > N) printf "    ... and %d more tool(s) carrying freestanding cut-points (MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TOP=%d)\n", n - N, N }'
  fi
  local nclosed; nclosed="$(grep -c '^CLOSEDTOOL ' <<<"$out")"
  if [ "$nclosed" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  semantically closed organs (every traceable cut-point derived from the mesh's own record):"
    grep '^CLOSEDTOOL ' <<<"$out" | sort -k3,3rn | awk '{ printf "    * %-24s SELF=%d\n", $2, $3 }'
  fi
}

# --- rendering ---
render() {
  local mode="$1"; shift
  local lines; lines="$(analyze "$SCRIPTS" "$CRON")" || return $?
  local core src peri unk loops
  core=$(  grep -c ' CORE$'       <<<"$lines"); src=$(grep -c ' SOURCE$'     <<<"$lines")
  peri=$(  grep -c ' PERIPHERAL$' <<<"$lines"); unk=$(grep -c ' UNKNOWN$'    <<<"$lines")
  loops=$( grep -c '^LOOP '       <<<"$lines")
  local total=$(( core + src + peri + unk ))
  case "$mode" in
    --peripheral) grep ' PERIPHERAL$' <<<"$lines" | awk '{print $1}' ;;
    --json)
      printf '{"wired":%d,"core":%d,"source":%d,"peripheral":%d,"unknown":%d,"loops":%d,' \
        "$total" "$core" "$src" "$peri" "$unk" "$loops"
      printf '"peripheral_list":['
      grep ' PERIPHERAL$' <<<"$lines" | awk '{print $1}' | paste -sd, - | sed 's/[^,]*/"&"/g'
      printf ']}\n' ;;
    *)
      echo "mesh-closure: organizational closure of the wired reflex set ($total reflexes)"
      echo "  CORE=$core (interior: consume AND are consumed)  SOURCE=$src (boundary producers)  LOOPS=$loops (closed pairs)"
      echo "  PERIPHERAL=$peri (wired+firing, but product feeds NO other constraint — advisory)  UNKNOWN=$unk"
      if [ "$peri" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "  peripheral candidates (judge each — an actuator's consumer is the world):"
        grep ' PERIPHERAL$' <<<"$lines" | awk '{print "    - "$1}'
      fi
      if [ "$loops" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "  closed constraint loops (mutual mesh-A<->mesh-B):"
        grep '^LOOP ' <<<"$lines" | awk '{print "    * "$2" <-> "$3}'
      fi ;;
  esac
}

# --- RED-first smoke test on a synthetic fixture graph ---
run_test() {
  local td; td="$(mktemp -d)"; trap 'rm -rf "$td"' RETURN
  local s="$td/scripts"; mkdir -p "$s"
  # fixture graph:  mesh-fa (raw sense) --> mesh-fb (fusion) <--> mesh-fc (mutual loop)
  #                 mesh-fd wired+present but mentioned by NOBODY  -> PERIPHERAL
  #                 mesh-fe wired but NO source file               -> UNKNOWN
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# a raw sense, consumes nothing\necho fa\n' > "$s/mesh-fa"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nx=$(mesh-fa); y=$(mesh-fc)\n'               > "$s/mesh-fb"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nz=$(mesh-fb)\n'                             > "$s/mesh-fc"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho fd; # names nobody\n'                  > "$s/mesh-fd"
  # a SECOND mutual loop, deliberately at MISMATCHED cadences -> SKEWED (so MATCHED/SKEWED are
  # both non-zero and the loop classifier is genuinely falsifiable, not green-by-vacuity)
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nq=$(mesh-fh)\n'                             > "$s/mesh-fg"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nq=$(mesh-fg)\n'                             > "$s/mesh-fh"
  # cadences are load-bearing for --timescale: fa regenerates every 2m, its only consumer fb reads
  # hourly (ALIASED 30x); fb<->fc are both hourly (CONSTRAINT both legs -> a MATCHED loop).
  local cron="$td/cron"
  printf '%s\n' \
    '*/2 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fa >> x 2>&1' \
    '0 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fb >> x 2>&1' \
    '0 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fc >> x 2>&1' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fd >> x 2>&1' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fg >> x 2>&1' \
    '0 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fh >> x 2>&1' \
    '# */5 * * * * a comment mentioning mesh-fa must NOT wire anything' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fe >> x 2>&1' > "$cron"

  local out fail=0
  out="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$s" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$cron" analyze "$s" "$cron")"
  chk() { grep -qx "$1" <<<"$out" || { echo "  FAIL: expected '$1'"; fail=1; }; }
  # fa: consumed by fb, consumes nothing -> SOURCE
  chk "mesh-fa SOURCE"
  # fb: consumes fa+fc AND consumed by fc -> CORE
  chk "mesh-fb CORE"
  # fc: consumes fb AND consumed by fb -> CORE   (and the loop)
  chk "mesh-fc CORE"
  chk "LOOP mesh-fb mesh-fc"
  # fd: present, wired, mentioned by nobody -> PERIPHERAL
  chk "mesh-fd PERIPHERAL"
  # fe: wired but no source file -> UNKNOWN
  chk "mesh-fe UNKNOWN"

  # RED-first falsifier: the classifier MUST distinguish PERIPHERAL from CORE. If it blindly called
  # everything CORE, mesh-fd would be CORE and the chk above would already be red. Prove the negative
  # directly: mesh-fd must NOT be CORE, and the peripheral render must list it.
  grep -qx "mesh-fd CORE" <<<"$out" && { echo "  FAIL: mesh-fd wrongly CORE (peripheral collapsed)"; fail=1; }
  local peri; peri="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$s" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$cron" bash "$0" --peripheral)"
  grep -qx "mesh-fd" <<<"$peri" || { echo "  FAIL: --peripheral did not list mesh-fd"; fail=1; }

  # ---- --timescale: the cron-period parser -------------------------------------------------
  # A separate cron fixture pinning every spec shape the real reflexes.cron actually uses. These
  # are the WEEKLY-MEAN periods the HONESTY BOUNDS promise, including the bursty dow case.
  local cron2="$td/cron2" cad
  printf '%s\n' \
    '6-59/30 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp1 >> x 2>&1' \
    '11-59/19 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp2 >> x 2>&1' \
    '17 3 * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp3 >> x 2>&1' \
    '19,49 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp4 >> x 2>&1' \
    '*/5 * * * 1 $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp5 >> x 2>&1' \
    '* * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp6 >> x 2>&1' \
    '@reboot $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fp7 --arg a b >> x 2>&1' > "$cron2"
  cad="$(cadences "$cron2" | awk '{printf "%s=%g ", $1, $2+0}')"
  cadchk() { case "$cad" in *"$1"*) ;; *) echo "  FAIL: cadence expected '$1' in [$cad]"; fail=1 ;; esac; }
  cadchk "mesh-fp1=30"     # 6,36 -> 2/h
  cadchk "mesh-fp2=20"     # 11,30,49 -> 3/h
  cadchk "mesh-fp3=1440"   # once daily
  cadchk "mesh-fp4=30"     # explicit list
  cadchk "mesh-fp5=35"     # every 5m but Mondays only -> WEEKLY MEAN, exactly as documented
  cadchk "mesh-fp6=1"      # every minute
  case "$cad" in *mesh-fp7*) echo "  FAIL: @reboot must declare no period"; fail=1 ;; esac

  # ---- --timescale: edge conservation + loop matching ---------------------------------------
  local ts
  ts="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$s" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$cron" bash "$0" --timescale)"
  tschk() { grep -qE "$1" <<<"$ts" || { echo "  FAIL: --timescale expected /$1/"; fail=1; }; }
  # edges: fa->fb (2m vs 60m) ALIASED 30x · fc->fb, fb->fc (60m/60m) CONSTRAINT · fg->fh (5m vs
  # 60m) ALIASED 12x · fh->fg (60m vs 5m) CONSTRAINT.  loops: fb<->fc MATCHED, fg<->fh SKEWED.
  tschk 'CONSTRAINT=3 '
  tschk 'ALIASED=2 '
  tschk 'MATCHED=1 .*SKEWED=1 '
  tschk 'mesh-fa \(2m\) -> mesh-fb \(60m\)   1 in 30'
  tschk 'mesh-fg \(5m\) -> mesh-fh \(60m\)   1 in 12'

  # RED-first falsifier for the NEW axis: the whole point is that a fast producer feeding a slow
  # consumer is NOT a conserved constraint. Collapse the comparison (always CONSTRAINT) and the
  # two asserts above go red — verified by hand: `tau[a] >= tau[b]` -> `1` yields
  # "CONSTRAINT=3 / ALIASED=0" and the worst-aliasing block disappears entirely. Prove the
  # negative directly here too: fa must NOT be counted conserved.
  grep -qE 'ALIASED=0 ' <<<"$ts" && { echo "  FAIL: aliasing collapsed (fa->fb read as conserved)"; fail=1; }
  # ...and the ratio floor must be a real knob, not a constant: at 40x, the 30x edge is MARGINAL.
  local ts2
  ts2="$(MESH_CLOSURE_ALIAS_MIN=40 MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$s" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$cron" bash "$0" --timescale)"
  grep -qE 'ALIASED=0 ' <<<"$ts2" || { echo "  FAIL: ALIAS_MIN=40 did not demote the 30x edge"; fail=1; }
  grep -qE 'MARGINAL=2 ' <<<"$ts2" || { echo "  FAIL: ALIAS_MIN=40 did not count the 30x/12x edges MARGINAL"; fail=1; }

  # ---- --enacted: the EXERCISE test on each edge ---------------------------------------------
  # Three more fixture reflexes, each mentioned by mesh-fb in exactly ONE kind of site:
  #   mesh-fi  only on a FULL-LINE COMMENT   -> PROSE-only edge   -> fi must DEMOTE to PERIPHERAL
  #   mesh-fj  only inside a test FUNCTION   -> TEST-only edge    -> fj must DEMOTE to PERIPHERAL
  #   mesh-fk  only inside parse_latest()    -> a live edge: "latest" must NOT read as a test fn
  # and a THIRD mutual pair (fl<->fm) whose fm->fl leg is prose-only: a loop with one unexercised
  # leg is not a closed constraint pair, so LOOPS must fall 3 -> 2.
  cat > "$s/mesh-fb" <<'FB'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
x=$(mesh-fa); y=$(mesh-fc)
# doc: this used to read mesh-fi, and the sentence outlives the code
parse_latest() {
  v=$(mesh-fk --latest)
}
do_test() {
  probe=$(mesh-fj --fixture)
}
z=1  # trailing comments are code: a mention here stays LIVE by design
FB
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho fi\n' > "$s/mesh-fi"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho fj\n' > "$s/mesh-fj"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho fk\n' > "$s/mesh-fk"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nq=$(mesh-fm)\n'                      > "$s/mesh-fl"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# fm once consumed mesh-fl\necho fm\n' > "$s/mesh-fm"
  local cron3="$td/cron3"
  { cat "$cron"
    printf '%s\n' \
      '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fi >> x 2>&1' \
      '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fj >> x 2>&1' \
      '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fk >> x 2>&1' \
      '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fl >> x 2>&1' \
      '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fm >> x 2>&1' \
      '*/7 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fd --gate && $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-fi >> x 2>&1'
  } > "$cron3"

  local en
  en="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$s" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$cron3" bash "$0" --enacted)"
  enchk() { grep -qE -- "$1" <<<"$en" || { echo "  FAIL: --enacted expected /$1/"; fail=1; }; }
  # the two unexercised edges are counted, and separated by SITE (prose vs test are not the same fault)
  enchk 'PROSE-only=2  TEST-only=1'          # fi (fb's comment) + fl (fm's comment) prose; fj test
  enchk 'UNEXERCISED=3 '
  # the demotions themselves — the load-bearing output
  # fi shares a cron line with mesh-fd, so its demotion is CAVEATED; fj is wired alone and is not.
  # Both edges of the marker are pinned: a marker applied to everything is the same as no marker.
  enchk '- mesh-fi   SOURCE -> PERIPHERAL   \[cron-chained\]'
  enchk '- mesh-fj   SOURCE -> PERIPHERAL$'
  enchk '1 of them are \[cron-chained\]'
  enchk '- mesh-fl   CORE -> PERIPHERAL'     # its only consumer named it in prose
  enchk 'mesh-fi -> mesh-fb   \[prose-only\]'
  enchk 'mesh-fj -> mesh-fb   \[test-only\]'
  # ...and a loop whose return leg is prose is NOT a closed constraint pair
  enchk 'LOOPS 3 -> 2'
  # RED-first falsifiers, each pinning a distinct half of the axis:
  # (a) a REAL code edge must survive — collapse "live" onto "all" and this stays green while the
  #     legs above go red; collapse it the other way (call every mention unexercised) and this one
  #     goes red on its own.
  grep -qE -- '(- mesh-fa |- mesh-fc )' <<<"$en" && { echo "  FAIL: a live code edge was demoted"; fail=1; }
  # (b) "latest" must not be read as a test function: fk is consumed inside parse_latest() ONLY.
  grep -qE -- '- mesh-fk ' <<<"$en" && { echo "  FAIL: parse_latest() read as a test harness (substring match)"; fail=1; }
  grep -qE 'mesh-fk -> ' <<<"$en" && { echo "  FAIL: mesh-fk edge counted unexercised"; fail=1; }
  # (c) the DECLARED graph must be untouched by all of this — same run, same cron, old flag.
  local dec3
  dec3="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$s" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$cron3" bash "$0" --peripheral)"
  grep -qx "mesh-fi" <<<"$dec3" && { echo "  FAIL: --peripheral (declared graph) changed: fi must be CORE-side there"; fail=1; }
  grep -qx "mesh-fd" <<<"$dec3" || { echo "  FAIL: --peripheral lost mesh-fd"; fail=1; }

  # ---- --null: which closure numbers can FAIL a degree-preserving null? -----------------------
  # (Nave 2026 — an unfalsifiable closure statistic measures the formalism, not the mesh.)
  # THREE fixtures. N1 and N2 share ONE degree sequence (every node: mentions 1, mentioned-by 1)
  # and differ only in how the mentions are wired:
  #     N1 reciprocal   n1<->n2 , n3<->n4        -> LOOPS 2
  #     N2 cycle        n1->n2->n3->n4->n1       -> LOOPS 0
  # so CORE/SOURCE/PERIPHERAL must be IDENTICAL across them while LOOPS moves. That equality is
  # the finding, driven through the real classifier — analyze() reads a class off two bits of a
  # node's own degree pair, so no rewiring at fixed degree can touch it.
  # N3 is the CONTROL that keeps the equality leg from being vacuous: a DIFFERENT degree sequence,
  # whose class counts must NOT match N1's. An equality assertion with no proven-unequal partner
  # asserts nothing.
  local nl="$td/nulls"; mkdir -p "$nl/n1" "$nl/n2" "$nl/n3"
  local ncron="$td/ncron"
  printf '%s\n' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-n1 >> x 2>&1' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-n2 >> x 2>&1' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-n3 >> x 2>&1' \
    '*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-n4 >> x 2>&1' > "$ncron"
  nfix() { printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nv=$(%s)\n' "$2" > "$1"; }
  nfix "$nl/n1/mesh-n1" 'mesh-n2'; nfix "$nl/n1/mesh-n2" 'mesh-n1'
  nfix "$nl/n1/mesh-n3" 'mesh-n4'; nfix "$nl/n1/mesh-n4" 'mesh-n3'
  nfix "$nl/n2/mesh-n1" 'mesh-n2'; nfix "$nl/n2/mesh-n2" 'mesh-n3'
  nfix "$nl/n2/mesh-n3" 'mesh-n4'; nfix "$nl/n2/mesh-n4" 'mesh-n1'
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\na=$(mesh-n2); b=$(mesh-n3)\n' > "$nl/n3/mesh-n1"
  nfix "$nl/n3/mesh-n2" 'mesh-n1'
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho n3\n' > "$nl/n3/mesh-n3"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho n4\n' > "$nl/n3/mesh-n4"

  nullrun() { MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$nl/$1" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$ncron" \
              MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_R="${2:-50}" bash "$0" --null; }
  clsline() { MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$nl/$1" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$ncron" bash "$0" \
              | grep -oE 'CORE=[0-9]+ .*LOOPS=[0-9]+'; }
  local nr1 nr2 nr3 c1 c2 c3
  nr1="$(nullrun n1)"; nr2="$(nullrun n2)"; nr3="$(nullrun n3)"
  c1="$(clsline n1)";  c2="$(clsline n2)";  c3="$(clsline n3)"

  # 1. the class counts are the SAME under two different wirings of one degree sequence...
  [ "${c1%%LOOPS*}" = "${c2%%LOOPS*}" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: class counts moved between two wirings of the same degree sequence: '$c1' vs '$c2'"; fail=1; }
  # 2. ...while LOOPS is genuinely different, so leg 1 is not comparing two identical graphs.
  grep -q 'LOOPS=2' <<<"$c1" || { echo "  FAIL: N1 (reciprocal) must show LOOPS=2, got '$c1'"; fail=1; }
  grep -q 'LOOPS=0' <<<"$c2" || { echo "  FAIL: N2 (cycle) must show LOOPS=0, got '$c2'"; fail=1; }
  # 3. CONTROL: a different degree sequence MUST break the equality, or leg 1 is unfalsifiable.
  [ "${c1%%LOOPS*}" = "${c3%%LOOPS*}" ] \
    && { echo "  FAIL: N3 has a different degree sequence but matched N1's classes — leg 1 is vacuous"; fail=1; }
  grep -q 'SOURCE=1' <<<"$c3" || { echo "  FAIL: N3 control must contain a SOURCE, got '$c3'"; fail=1; }

  # 4. the null must actually REWIRE: if the swap were a no-op the null mean would equal the
  #    observed 2.00 exactly. It must be strictly below it.
  grep -qE 'declared .*LOOPS observed=2 .*null mean=2\.00' <<<"$nr1" \
    && { echo "  FAIL: --null swap is a no-op (null mean == observed)"; fail=1; }
  grep -qE 'declared .*LOOPS observed=2 .*null mean=[01]\.' <<<"$nr1" \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --null did not report a below-observed null mean on the reciprocal fixture"; fail=1; }
  grep -qE 'declared .*LOOPS observed=0 ' <<<"$nr2" \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --null observed LOOPS on the cycle fixture must be 0"; fail=1; }

  # 5. seeded => reproducible. A null whose number moves on an unchanged graph is a bug, and every
  #    reading of it downstream would be unrepeatable.
  [ "$nr1" = "$(nullrun n1)" ] || { echo "  FAIL: --null is not reproducible at a fixed seed"; fail=1; }
  # ...and the seed is REALLY consumed: a different seed must move at least one replicate stream.
  local nr1b; nr1b="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$nl/n1" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$ncron" \
                      MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_R=50 MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SEED=99991 bash "$0" --null)"
  [ "$nr1b" = "$nr1" ] && { echo "  FAIL: MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_SEED is ignored (identical output)"; fail=1; }

  # 6. R is echoed and honoured: the p floor is 1/(R+1), so a reader can never mistake the floor
  #    for a measured zero.
  grep -q 'R=50 replicates' <<<"$nr1" || { echo "  FAIL: --null did not echo the replicate count"; fail=1; }
  grep -qE 'R=7 replicates' <<<"$(nullrun n1 7)" || { echo "  FAIL: MESH_CLOSURE_NULL_R is ignored"; fail=1; }
  # 7. the invariance summary must print the REAL class counts, not a canned string.
  grep -qE 'declared 4 0 0 0 ' <<<"$nr1" \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --null invariance summary did not carry N1's actual class counts"; fail=1; }
  grep -qE 'declared 2 1 1 0 ' <<<"$nr3" \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --null invariance summary is canned (N3 printed N1's counts)"; fail=1; }
  # 8. a graph too small to swap must say so, never invent a null.
  local ntiny="$td/ntiny"; mkdir -p "$ntiny"
  nfix "$ntiny/mesh-n1" 'mesh-n2'; nfix "$ntiny/mesh-n2" 'mesh-n1'
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho n3\n' > "$ntiny/mesh-n3"
  printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho n4\n' > "$ntiny/mesh-n4"
  # captured, never piped into `grep -q`: an early-exiting grep SIGPIPEs the python and pipefail
  # turns a correct report into rc=141 (plus a BrokenPipeError on stderr).
  local ntout
  ntout="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$ntiny" MESH_CLOSURE_CRON="$ncron" bash "$0" --null)"
  grep -qE 'null=n/a \(fewer than 4 edges to swap\)' <<<"$ntout" \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --null must render n/a (not a fabricated null) on a 2-edge graph"; fail=1; }

  # ---- --symmetry (a): the ESTIMATOR, against four synthetic series of KNOWN direction ---------
  # A bivariate AR(1) pair with a single cross-lag switched on/off. This is the falsifier that
  # matters for a causality claim: the same code must produce FOUR DIFFERENT verdicts, so an
  # estimator that is stuck on any one of them (or that ignores direction) goes red on three legs.
  local gen="$td/gen.awk"
  cat > "$gen" <<'GENAWK'
BEGIN { seed = 12345; n = 150
  # cross-lag coefficients. The MUTUAL case must stay STABLE (spectral radius 0.3+c < 1), or the
  # two series explode into collinearity and the second regression degenerates to a false "no
  # coupling" — which is how this leg first went red, not a property of the estimator.
  cs = (MODE == "a2s") ? 0.9 : (MODE == "mut") ? 0.45 : 0     # activity -> structure
  ca = (MODE == "s2a") ? 0.9 : (MODE == "mut") ? 0.45 : 0     # structure -> activity
  lag = (MODE == "lag3") ? 3 : 1                              # deep-lag coupling (AIC leg)
  if (MODE == "lag3") ca = 0.9
  if (MODE == "diu") {                                        # 4-periodic COMMON DRIVE, no coupling
    split("0.0 1.7 0.4 -1.2", D, " "); split("0.9 -0.6 1.4 0.2", E, " ")
    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) printf "%d %.6f %.6f\n", i, D[((i-1)%4)+1] + 0.4*rnd(), E[((i-1)%4)+1] + 0.4*rnd()
    exit }
  for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { es[i] = rnd(); ea[i] = rnd() }
  s[1] = es[1]; a[1] = ea[1]
  for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    s[i] = 0.3 * s[i-1] + es[i] + cs * a[i-1]
    a[i] = 0.3 * a[i-1] + ea[i] + (i > lag ? ca * s[i-lag] : 0) }
  for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) printf "%d %.6f %.6f\n", i, s[i], a[i] }
function rnd() { seed = (1103515245 * seed + 12345) % 2147483648; return (seed / 2147483648.0) - 0.5 }
GENAWK
  local mode want got
  # lag3 couples S->A at lag THREE only: it is the leg that makes AIC lag selection load-bearing
  # (pin L=1 and the estimator cannot see the coupling at all -> DECOUPLED).
  for mode in "s2a STRUCTURE-DRIVEN" "a2s ACTIVITY-DRIVEN" "mut SYMMETRIZED" "ind DECOUPLED" \
              "lag3 STRUCTURE-DRIVEN"; do
    set -- $mode
    awk -v MODE="$1" -f "$gen" > "$td/sym_$1"
    # BIN_H=24 -> one bin per day -> no deseasoning, so this leg asserts the estimator ALONE
    got="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES="$td/sym_$1" MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_BIN_H=24 bash "$0" --symmetry \
           | awk '/^  VERDICT:/ {print $2}')"
    [ "$got" = "$2" ] || { echo "  FAIL: --symmetry on a known-$1 series said '$got', want '$2'"; fail=1; }
  done

  # A shared DIURNAL cycle must not read as coupling. The circular-shift null does NOT save you here
  # (a shift by a whole period reproduces the alignment, but the in-between shifts do not, so the
  # null stays low): measured on this very fixture, deseasoning off gives g(S->A)=0.323 p=0.013 and
  # a confident, entirely spurious STRUCTURE-DRIVEN. On it: g=0.013 p=0.19, DECOUPLED. Both the
  # verdict and the magnitude are pinned below, because both move.
  awk -v MODE=diu -f "$gen" > "$td/sym_diu"
  local diu dg
  diu="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES="$td/sym_diu" MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_BIN_H=6 bash "$0" --symmetry)"
  grep -q 'VERDICT: DECOUPLED' <<<"$diu" || { echo "  FAIL: a pure diurnal common drive read as coupling"; fail=1; }
  dg="$(awk '/g\(structure->activity\)/ {print $3}' <<<"$diu")"
  awk -v g="$dg" 'BEGIN { exit !(g < 0.05) }' \
    || { echo "  FAIL: diurnal cycle not removed — g(S->A)=$dg on an uncoupled fixture"; fail=1; }

  # ---- --symmetry (b): the SERIES BUILDER, from a raw git-churn fixture -----------------------
  # Injecting a ready-made series above bypasses the parser entirely, so drive the whole path from
  # a `git log --numstat` stream with a HAND-COMPUTED answer: rename chaining, the lambda=0.8
  # relocation credit, binary diffs, the tau horizon, and the leakage shift are each pinned by a
  # line only they can produce.  tau=2h, bins=1h -> Gamma is published 2 bins after the edit.
  local glog="$td/gitlog" T=1800000000
  { printf 'C %d\n1\t0\tfileD\n'                       $((T + 20*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n-\t-\tbin.dat\n'                     $((T + 12*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n0\t0\tfileG => fileH\n'              $((T + 10*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n7\t0\tfileC\n6\t0\tfileG\n'          $((T +  9*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n1\t1\tfileE => fileF\n'              $((T +  6*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n2\t0\tfileB\n4\t0\tfileE\n'          $((T +  5*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n3\t1\tfileA\n'                       $((T +  1*3600))
    printf 'C %d\n10\t0\tfileA\n5\t0\tfileB\n'         $((T +  0*3600))
  } > "$glog"
  local ser
  ser="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG="$glog" MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_TAU_H=2 MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_BIN_H=1 \
         bash "$0" --symmetry-series)"
  serchk() { grep -qx "$1" <<<"$ser" || { echo "  FAIL: series expected '$1'"; fail=1; }; }
  serchk "2 0.500000 0.000000"    # fileA@0h rewritten at 1h -> 0 ; fileB@0h survives -> 1 ; mean .5
  serchk "7 0.500000 0.000000"    # fileE@5h dies at the RENAME 6h -> chain resolved (else it'd be 1.0)
  serchk "11 0.900000 0.000000"   # fileG@9h relocated with no churn at 10h -> lambda 0.8 ; mean .9
  serchk "9 1.000000 2.639057"    # activity = log1p(7+6) in the edit's OWN bin
  serchk "12 1.000000 0.000000"   # a binary diff ("- -") counts as zero churn, not a parse error
  # THE LEAKAGE GUARD: Gamma for the 0h edits must appear at bin 2 (horizon close), never at bin 0.
  # Delete the `+ shift` in sym_series and these two go red while every other leg stays green —
  # and on live history that same deletion flips the verdict ACTIVITY-DRIVEN -> SYMMETRIZED.
  grep -qE '^[01] ' <<<"$ser" && { echo "  FAIL: Gamma published before its horizon closed (leak)"; fail=1; }
  [ "$(grep -c . <<<"$ser")" = 19 ] || { echo "  FAIL: expected 19 series bins (2..20), got $(grep -c . <<<"$ser")"; fail=1; }

  # ---- --symmetry (c): honest n/a, and STRICT fixtures ----------------------------------------
  # Too few bins must yield UNDETERMINED, not a verdict manufactured from 20. 20 is deliberate: it
  # is long enough for the regressions to SUCCEED, so this leg fails only if the MIN_N floor itself
  # is gone (at 5 points the regression is singular and would render UNDETERMINED for free).
  head -20 "$td/sym_mut" > "$td/sym_short"
  MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES="$td/sym_short" bash "$0" --symmetry | grep -q 'UNDETERMINED' \
    || { echo "  FAIL: a 5-bin series did not render UNDETERMINED"; fail=1; }
  # A fixture var pointing at nothing must ERROR, never silently fall back to the live repo.
  if MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_GITLOG="$td/no-such-fixture" bash "$0" --symmetry >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  FAIL: unreadable SYM_GITLOG fixture fell through to live git"; fail=1; fi
  if MESH_CLOSURE_SYM_SERIES="$td/no-such-fixture" bash "$0" --symmetry >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  FAIL: unreadable SYM_SERIES fixture fell through to live git"; fail=1; fi

  # ---- --semantic: PROVENANCE of every decision threshold -------------------------------------
  # Its own scripts dir: the graph fixtures above must not perturb the site census, and vice versa.
  local sem="$td/sem"; mkdir -p "$sem"
  # fs1  SELF      — the cut-point is read back out of the mesh's own record at runtime
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'T=$(mesh-fa --p95)' '[ "$x" -gt "$T" ] && echo hi' > "$sem/mesh-fs1"
  # fs2  IMPOSED x2 — a bare literal, and one inside (( )); plus a PROSE line that must not count
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' '[ "$x" -gt 42 ] && echo a' '(( x > 7 )) && echo b' \
                '# a commented threshold [ "$x" -gt 99 ] is prose, never a site' > "$sem/mesh-fs2"
  # fs3  TRIVIAL x2 (0 and 1 are presence tests) + IMPOSED x1 via a ${ENV:-<num>} default
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' '[ "$#" -gt 0 ] || exit 2' '[ "$n" -gt 1 ] && echo many' \
                'LIM="${MESH_X_LIM:-9}"' '[ "$n" -ge "$LIM" ] && echo over' > "$sem/mesh-fs3"
  # fs4  UNRESOLVED — a command substitution that reads no mesh record: honest n/a, never a class
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'V=$(some_fn)' '[ "$x" -lt "$V" ] && echo c' > "$sem/mesh-fs4"
  # fs5  OPAQUE   — no shell-visible site at all, but the interpreter lives in an embedded awk body
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'echo x | awk "{ if (\$1 > 3) print }"' > "$sem/mesh-fs5"
  # fs6  a test-harness body is NOT the organ: only the production cut-point (55) is an interpreter.
  # The harness deliberately writes a fixture whose TEXT holds a column-0 `name() {` ... `}` pair —
  # a depth-blind skip ends at that inner `}` and lets 77 in. This is the real shape the live census
  # hit (mesh-closure's own --test) and the only leg that falsifies the brace-DEPTH counting.
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' '[ "$x" -gt 55 ] && echo live' 'run_test() {' \
                '  cat > /dev/null <<EOF' 'parse_x() {' '  v=1' '}' 'EOF' \
                '  [ "$x" -gt 77 ] && echo fixture' '}' > "$sem/mesh-fs6"
  # fs7  the read is usually ONE HOP away: the site says $(band), the BODY touches the record, and
  # the band is then aliased on a compound `A="$B"; C=…` line. A scan that stops at the assignment
  # RHS calls this UNRESOLVED and reports a self-calibrated organ as untraceable (live shape:
  # mesh-node-health's cadence-derived lease, mesh-stress's band block).
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'band() {' '  cut -d= -f2 "$HOME/.mesh/therm.log" | tail -1' '}' \
                'WARM_ABS="${MESH_FS7_WARM:-$(band)}"' 'WARM="$WARM_ABS"; SRC=absolute' \
                '[ "$t" -gt "$WARM" ] && echo hot' > "$sem/mesh-fs7"
  # fs8  the LEDGER hop is not closure: `--const` returns a number a human wrote into a data row.
  # Without this leg the hop above mints SELF for all 20 threshold-ledger consumers at once.
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'led() {' '  mesh-sexpr-gate --const fs8-gate warm 90' '}' \
                'LIMIT="${MESH_FS8_WARM:-$(led)}"' '[ "$t" -ge "$LIMIT" ] && echo over' > "$sem/mesh-fs8"
  # fs9  a one-line function ENDING IN A COMMENT: an ends-in-`}` rule reads it as multi-line and
  # swallows the NEXT function whole, so a pure mapper inherits its neighbour's record read and
  # reports SELF off borrowed provenance (live shape: mesh-situation rank() / situation_decide()).
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'rankit(){ case "$1" in HI)echo 3;; *)echo 0;; esac; }  # 0 = UNKNOWN' \
                'reader() {' '  grep -c x "$HOME/.mesh/room.log"' '}' 'N=$(rankit "$s")' \
                '[ "$n" -gt "$N" ] && echo up' > "$sem/mesh-fs9"
  # fs10  a value READ at runtime, then guarded against empty by `${hi:-0}`. The read is not an `=`
  # assignment, so the guard was the only visible provenance and a measured quantity filed as a
  # freestanding constant — an IMPOSED that inflates the denominator with the metric own blind spot.
  printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' 'out=$(get_it)' 'read -r lo hi <<<"$out"; hi="${hi:-0}"' \
                '[ "$n" -gt "$hi" ] && echo over' > "$sem/mesh-fs10"

  local semsites
  semsites="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$sem" bash "$0" --semantic-sites)"
  semn() { grep -c " $1 " <<<"$semsites"; }
  local sc_self sc_imp sc_unr sc_triv sc_opq
  sc_self=$(semn SELF); sc_imp=$(semn IMPOSED); sc_unr=$(semn UNRESOLVED)
  sc_triv=$(semn TRIVIAL); sc_opq=$(semn OPAQUE)
  semchk() { [ "$2" = "$3" ] || { echo "  FAIL: --semantic $1 expected $3, got $2"; fail=1; }; }
  semchk SELF "$sc_self" 2; semchk IMPOSED "$sc_imp" 5; semchk UNRESOLVED "$sc_unr" 3
  semchk TRIVIAL "$sc_triv" 2; semchk OPAQUE "$sc_opq" 1
  # EXACT SET, not just counts: a class landing on the wrong tool would keep every count above green.
  local semexp
  semexp="$(printf '%s\n' 'mesh-fs1 SELF "$T"' 'mesh-fs2 IMPOSED 42' 'mesh-fs2 IMPOSED 7' \
                          'mesh-fs3 IMPOSED "$LIM"' 'mesh-fs3 TRIVIAL 0' 'mesh-fs3 TRIVIAL 1' \
                          'mesh-fs4 UNRESOLVED "$V"' 'mesh-fs5 OPAQUE -' \
                          'mesh-fs6 IMPOSED 55' 'mesh-fs7 SELF "$WARM"' \
                          'mesh-fs8 IMPOSED "$LIMIT"' 'mesh-fs9 UNRESOLVED "$N"' \
                          'mesh-fs10 UNRESOLVED "$hi"' | sort)"
  diff <(sort <<<"$semsites") <(printf '%s\n' "$semexp") >/dev/null \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --semantic site set differs from the fixture expectation:"
         diff <(sort <<<"$semsites") <(printf '%s\n' "$semexp") | sed 's/^/    /'; fail=1; }
  local semrep
  semrep="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$sem" bash "$0" --semantic)"
  semgrep() { grep -qE "$1" <<<"$semrep" || { echo "  FAIL: --semantic expected /$1/"; fail=1; }; }
  semgrep 'SCI = SELF/\(SELF\+IMPOSED\) = 0\.2857 '   # 2 SELF of 7 assessable (TRIVIAL excluded)
  semgrep 'rung: PARTIAL'
  semgrep 'OPAQUE=1 tools'
  semgrep '\* mesh-fs1 +SELF=1'                       # the one organ with no freestanding cut-point
  semgrep '\* mesh-fs1 +operand "\$T"'                # every SELF site is listed, never just counted
  semgrep '\- mesh-fs2 +IMPOSED=2 '
  # RED-first falsifiers, each verified by hand against this fixture:
  #  (1) drop the TRIVIAL set (opclass returns IMPOSED for 0/1): TRIVIAL 2->0, IMPOSED 3->5, and
  #      SCI 0.2500 -> 0.1667. Both the count legs and the SCI leg go red.
  #  (2) collapse kindof()'s command-substitution arm to "IMPOSED": SELF 1->0, IMPOSED 3->4,
  #      SCI -> 0.0000, rung PARTIAL -> IMPOSED, and the closed-organ line disappears.
  #  (3) delete the `^[ \t]*#` skip in pass 2: fs2's prose threshold 99 becomes a 4th IMPOSED site.
  # Prove the ones that can be driven from OUTSIDE the code directly, so the assertions are not
  # merely asserted-in-a-comment: the TRIVIAL set is an env knob, so leg (1) is executable.
  local semtriv
  semtriv="$(MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$sem" MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TRIVIAL="" bash "$0" --semantic-sites)"
  [ "$(grep -c ' TRIVIAL ' <<<"$semtriv")" = 0 ] && [ "$(grep -c ' IMPOSED ' <<<"$semtriv")" = 7 ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: emptying MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TRIVIAL did not move 0/1 into IMPOSED"; fail=1; }
  # ... and with the trivial set gone the headline MUST move: a metric that survives its own
  # exclusion rule being deleted was never measuring the exclusion.
  MESH_CLOSURE_SCRIPTS="$sem" MESH_CLOSURE_SEM_TRIVIAL="" bash "$0" --semantic \
    | grep -q '= 0\.2857 ' && { echo "  FAIL: SCI unchanged with the TRIVIAL exclusion removed"; fail=1; }

  if [ "$fail" -eq 0 ]; then echo "mesh-closure --test: PASS"; else echo "mesh-closure --test: FAIL"; return 1; fi
}

case "${1:-}" in
  --test)        run_test ;;
  --peripheral)  render --peripheral ;;
  --timescale)   render_timescale ;;
  --enacted)     render_enacted ;;
  --null)        render_null ;;
  --symmetry)    render_symmetry ;;
  --symmetry-series) sym_series ;;
  --semantic)    render_semantic ;;
  --semantic-sites) sem_sites "$SCRIPTS" ;;
  --cadences)    cadences "$CRON" ;;
  --json)        render --json ;;
  ''|--full)     render --full ;;
  -h|--help)     grep -E '^#( |$)' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//' | head -40 ;;
  *) echo "mesh-closure: unknown arg '$1' (try --test|--peripheral|--timescale|--enacted|--null|--symmetry|--semantic|--cadences|--json)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
